      title: 'Tempura: The Alchemy of Batter',
      subtitle: 'How Portugal gave Japan its most delicate fry',
      description: '''Tempura was introduced to Japan by Portuguese missionaries in the 16th century but was transformed into something uniquely Japanese—perhaps the world's lightest, most delicate fried food.

The secret of great tempura batter:
- **Ice-cold water**: The dramatic temperature difference between cold batter and hot oil creates the characteristic light, lacy coating
- **Under-mixing**: Leaving lumps in the batter is intentional—overmixing develops gluten and makes the coating heavy
- **Immediate frying**: Batter is made in small batches and used immediately, never left to sit
- **Oil temperature control**: Different ingredients fry at different temperatures—shrimp need very hot oil; vegetables need slightly cooler

Classic tempura ingredients: tiger prawns (ebi), Japanese eggplant (nasu), green pepper (piman), sweet potato (satsumaimo), lotus root (renkon), and shiso leaf. Each is eaten immediately after frying—tempura waits for no one.

The dipping sauce (tentsuyu) is a balance of dashi, mirin, and soy sauce, served with finely grated daikon radish that cleanses the palate between bites.

High-end tempura restaurants (tenchiku) seat guests at the counter where they watch the chef fry each piece to order and serve it directly—a performance as much as a meal.''',
