      title: 'Miso: Japan\'s Living Condiment',
      subtitle: 'Fermented soybeans and 1,300 years of umami',
      description: '''Miso—fermented soybean paste—is one of Japan's oldest and most fundamental foods, predating even rice cultivation in some regions. Understanding miso is understanding the backbone of Japanese cuisine.

Types of miso:
- **Shiro miso (white)**: Mild and sweet, aged only weeks. Kyoto-style, used in delicate soups and dressings
- **Aka miso (red)**: Deeply fermented, bold and salty. Used in hearty soups, especially in Nagoya's distinctive cuisine
- **Hatcho miso**: The darkest, most intense variety, fermented 2-3 years under enormous weights. Only made in Okazaki, Aichi Prefecture
- **Mugi miso**: Made with barley instead of rice, earthier flavor

Miso is a "living" food—it contains active koji mold (Aspergillus oryzae) that continues to develop in the jar. Never boil miso soup; add miso after the pot leaves the heat to preserve its live cultures and complex flavor.

Making miso at home is a centuries-old tradition. Families often prepare their annual batch in winter, then age it through the seasons. The result, called "te-mae miso" (handmade miso), is considered the finest.

Miso contains all essential amino acids and is rich in vitamins B, E, and K. Multiple studies suggest regular consumption supports gut health and may reduce cancer risk.''',
