    CultureContent(
      id: 'washoku_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ET5KQOae8XM',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Washoku: The UNESCO-Recognized Food Culture',
      subtitle: 'Balance, seasons, and the philosophy of the Japanese table',
      description: '''In 2013, UNESCO recognized washoku (Japanese food culture) as Intangible Cultural Heritage—not for specific dishes, but for the entire way of eating and relating to food.

The principles of washoku:
- **Ichiju sansai**: "One soup, three sides"—the traditional meal structure with rice, miso soup, and three side dishes. The balance of salt, sweet, sour, bitter, and umami
- **Umami**: The fifth taste (in addition to sweet, salty, sour, bitter)—a savory, satisfying depth achieved through dashi (stock from kombu seaweed and dried fish)
- **Shun**: Eating ingredients at the peak of their season
- **Teinei**: Careful, respectful handling of every ingredient, even garnishes

Japanese food plating follows specific rules:
- Odd numbers are auspicious; plates typically have 3, 5, or 7 pieces
- Different shapes of plates are used for different foods
- The arrangement should suggest the natural landscape

The five colors (white, black, red, yellow, green) and five cooking methods (raw, boiled, baked, fried, steamed) should ideally appear in a complete meal.

Itadakimasu ("I humbly receive") before eating and gochisosama deshita ("it was a feast") after are expressions of gratitude to everyone involved in producing the food—farmers, fishers, cooks, and the natural world.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1547592180-85f173990554?w=800',
      tags: ['washoku', 'food culture', 'UNESCO', 'ichiju sansai', 'balance'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 8),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_cuisine',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'wagashi_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81FwQEYre8o',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Wagashi: Edible Poetry',
      subtitle: 'Seasonal sweets that celebrate nature\'s beauty',
      description: '''Wagashi are traditional Japanese confections that are as much visual art as food. Created to accompany the bitter taste of matcha tea, they represent the seasons in miniature edible form.

Types of wagashi:
- **Namagashi**: Fresh sweets made from mochi or anko (sweet red bean paste), shaped to represent the current season—a tiny maple leaf in autumn, a cherry blossom in spring, a morning glory in summer
- **Higashi**: Dry confections made from rice flour and sugar, often pressed into seasonal molds
- **Yokan**: Dense, jellied sweet bean paste, often given as prestigious gifts
- **Daifuku**: Round mochi stuffed with sweet bean paste
- **Dorayaki**: Two pancakes sandwiching sweet red bean filling (also Doraemon's favorite)

The seasonal calendar dictates wagashi:
- January: Kadomatsu (pine decoration shape), celebrating New Year
- March: Sakura (cherry blossom) shapes for Hinamatsuri
- October: Chestnuts (kuri) and chrysanthemums for autumn

Skilled wagashi artisans (kashikan) train for years to perfect the techniques of kneading, sculpting, and coloring. The best confectioneries have been making the same recipes for hundreds of years.

Famous wagashi cities: Kyoto (home of the most refined tradition) and Kanazawa (second to Kyoto in confectionery culture).''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1558618666-fcd25c85cd64?w=800',
      tags: ['wagashi', 'sweets', 'matcha', 'seasonal', 'art'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 7),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wagashi',
    ),

