    CultureContent(
      id: 'kyoto_temples_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aansXcMqnNk',
      categoryId: 'region',
      title: 'Kyoto: The Temple City',
      subtitle: 'Ancient capital with over 2,000 temples',
      description: '''Kyoto served as Japan's imperial capital for over 1,000 years and remains the spiritual heart of Japanese culture. With more than 2,000 Buddhist temples and Shinto shrines, it's a living museum of traditional Japan.

Famous sites:
- **Fushimi Inari**: Thousands of vermillion torii gates creating tunnels through the forest, donated by businesses praying for success
- **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)**: A gold-covered temple reflected in a mirror pond, rebuilt after a Buddhist novice burned the original out of obsessive love for it
- **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove**: A serene path through towering bamboo
- **Gion**: The geisha district with traditional wooden machiya townhouses

Kyoto's 17 UNESCO World Heritage Sites form a ring around the city. The old street grid (modeled on the Tang Dynasty Chinese capital Changan) means neighborhoods are geographically distinct: the east for temples, the north for aristocratic history, the west for bamboo and Zen.

The geiko (Kyoto dialect for geisha) and maiko (apprentice geiko) culture of Gion remains active. Genuine sightings in Hanamikoji-dori alley are possible at dusk.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1540959375944-7049f642e9d2?w=800',
      tags: ['kyoto', 'temples', 'history', 'geisha', 'heritage'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 14),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyoto',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'okinawa_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HB5k9Ohzk0U',
      categoryId: 'region',
      title: 'Okinawa: A Kingdom Apart',
      subtitle: 'The Ryukyu Island chain\'s unique culture and longevity secret',
      description: '''Okinawa was not part of Japan until 1879—it was the independent Ryukyu Kingdom for nearly 500 years, trading with China, Southeast Asia, and Japan. This history makes its culture distinctively different from mainland Japan.

Distinctive Okinawan culture:
- **Ryukyuan language**: Closely related to Japanese but mutually unintelligible—a separate language rather than a dialect
- **Sanshin**: A three-stringed instrument (snake-skin shamisen) central to Okinawan music, totally different from mainland Japanese music
- **Eisa**: A communal drum dance performed during Obon festival, expressing both Buddhist reverence and ancestral connection
- **Shuri Castle**: The coral-stone castle of the Ryukyu kings, painted a distinctive red (Chinese influence), rebuilt after WWII destruction

Okinawa is famous as a "Blue Zone"—one of the world's five regions with exceptional longevity. The Okinawan diet traditionally included:
- Purple sweet potato (beni-imo) as the main carbohydrate
- Abundant vegetables (goya bitter melon, especially)
- Tofu in large quantities
- Pork (but the whole animal—including feet, ears, blood)
- Small meals with "hara hachi bu" (eat until 80% full)

The Battle of Okinawa (1945) killed one-third of the civilian population. This trauma shapes Okinawan identity and its relationship with Japan and the US military presence.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1480796927426-f609979314bd?w=800',
      tags: ['okinawa', 'ryukyu', 'island', 'longevity', 'unique culture'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 13),
      readTime: 7,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Okinawa_Prefecture',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'tokyo_neighborhoods_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dgfBlLR9ppw',
      categoryId: 'region',
      title: 'Tokyo\'s Hidden Villages Within a Megacity',
      subtitle: 'How 13 million people live in overlapping worlds',
      description: '''Tokyo is the world's largest metropolitan area, yet it functions less like a single city and more like dozens of distinct villages that happen to share train lines. Each neighborhood has its own character, subculture, and unwritten rules.

Key neighborhoods and their identities:
- **Yanaka**: The most "old Tokyo" neighborhood, survived WWII bombings, full of centuries-old temples, traditional craftspeople, and cats
- **Shimokitazawa**: Bohemian hub of vintage clothing, independent theater, live jazz, and second-hand bookstores
- **Akihabara**: Electronics and anime paradise—but also home to Japan's maid cafe culture and the visible face of otaku subculture
- **Daikanyama**: The "Brooklyn of Tokyo"—design boutiques, architecture-forward cafes, and curated lifestyle
- **Koenji**: Alternative Tokyo—punk record stores, vintage shops, and indie music venues

The shitamachi (downtown, "low city") vs. yamanote (uptown, "high city") distinction goes back to Edo period. Shitamachi (Asakusa, Ueno) retains working-class warmth and directness; Yamanote (Shibuya, Shinjuku) is polished, fashionable, cosmopolitan.

Tokyo's train system is key to understanding its geography—neighborhoods cluster around station areas. Each major station can have 200+ shops, restaurants, and services underground.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1540959375944-7049f642e9d2?w=800',
      tags: ['tokyo', 'neighborhoods', 'city', 'urban culture', 'shitamachi'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 12),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tokyo',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'hokkaido_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o_z4rlbHBqE',
      categoryId: 'region',
      title: 'Hokkaido: Japan\'s Wild North',
      subtitle: 'Indigenous Ainu culture, volcanic peaks, and powder snow',
      description: '''Hokkaido, Japan's northernmost main island, was only settled by the Japanese government in the Meiji era (1868 onward)—before then it was home to the Ainu people, Japan's indigenous inhabitants.

The Ainu people:
- Distinct ethnicity from mainland Japanese, descended from the ancient Jomon people; genetic research shows them to be of East/Northeast Asian origin, closely related to the Ryukyuan people of Okinawa
- Complex spiritual system centered on kamuy (spirits in nature)—bear ceremonies (iyomante) were the most elaborate, treating bears as divine messengers
- Ainu embroidery is distinguished by swirling patterns that protect against evil
- Ainu language is a language isolate—not related to Japanese or any other language

Hokkaido's natural character:
- **Volcanic landscape**: Multiple active volcanoes including Mt. Usu and Showa Shinzan (born 1943)
- **Powder snow**: Niseko and Furano are world-famous ski destinations with deep, dry powder
- **Flower fields**: Biei and Furano's lavender and sunflower fields in summer create patchwork landscape
- **Wildlife**: Brown bears (Ussuri), Blakiston's fish owl, red-crowned cranes—species extinct on Honshu

Hokkaido cuisine reflects the cold climate: miso ramen with butter and corn, Genghis Khan grilled lamb, dairy products from vast farms, fresh crab and sea urchin (uni) from cold Okhotsk Sea waters.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1480796927426-f609979314bd?w=800',
      tags: ['hokkaido', 'ainu', 'indigenous', 'nature', 'snow'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 11),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hokkaido',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'hiroshima_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9hXNLA0UghA',
      categoryId: 'region',
      title: 'Hiroshima: Rebirth and Peace',
      subtitle: 'A city that transformed tragedy into a message for humanity',
      description: '''Hiroshima is simultaneously a symbol of the worst of human destruction and the most powerful expression of hope for peace. On August 6, 1945, the first atomic bomb killed between 80,000-140,000 people. Today, Hiroshima is a thriving, peaceful city with a profound responsibility.

The Peace Memorial Park and Museum:
- Built at the epicenter of the bomb's impact, the park contains the Atomic Bomb Dome (Genbaku Dome)—the only structure left standing near the hypocenter, preserved as a reminder
- The Children's Peace Monument honors Sadako Sasaki, a 12-year-old who folded 1,000 paper cranes (senbazuru) believing it would heal her radiation-caused leukemia. She died before completing them. Now over 10 million paper cranes are sent annually from around the world
- The museum presents the human scale of the bombing through personal artifacts, photographs, and testimonies

The hibakusha (atomic bomb survivors) continue to share their stories, many well into their 90s. Their testimonies—of searching for family members in rubble, surviving with lifelong health effects, and choosing to speak for peace rather than revenge—are extraordinarily moving.

Hiroshima is also famous for its distinct okonomiyaki (savory pancake)—the Hiroshima style layers ingredients rather than mixing them, topped with noodles. Miyajima island, with its iconic floating torii gate, is 30 minutes away.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1492571350019-22de08371fd3?w=800',
      tags: ['hiroshima', 'peace', 'history', 'WWII', 'memorial'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 10),
      readTime: 7,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiroshima',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'kyushu_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XiltwQ2Ppbw',
      categoryId: 'region',
      title: 'Kyushu: Where Japan Meets the World',
      subtitle: 'Hot springs, ancient trade, and the gateway to China',
      description: '''Kyushu, Japan's southwestern main island, was Japan's main point of contact with the outside world for centuries. Through the port of Nagasaki, all Western trade flowed—books, science, medicine, firearms, and Christianity.

Key Kyushu cultures and experiences:
- **Nagasaki**: A city shaped by 200+ years of being Japan's only legal trading port with the outside world. Dutch merchants confined to the artificial island Dejima brought astronomy, medicine, and Western learning. Today, Nagasaki Chinatown and Glover Garden (a 19th-century Scottish merchant's home) reflect this cosmopolitan heritage. Also a second atomic bomb target.
- **Fukuoka (Hakata)**: Japan's closest city to South Korea, with its own dialect (Hakata-ben), food culture (tonkotsu ramen originated here), and famous Yamakasa festival where teams race through the streets carrying elaborate floats
- **Beppu**: The world's highest concentration of geothermal hot springs—so many onsen that steam rises from the streets. The "hells" (jigoku) are not for bathing but viewing—one pool is blood-red, another contains crocodiles
- **Asozan**: One of the world's largest volcanic calderas—the entire Aso valley is inside it, with 50,000 people living within a caldera formed 90,000 years ago''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1566073771259-6a8506099945?w=800',
      tags: ['kyushu', 'nagasaki', 'fukuoka', 'history', 'trade'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 9),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyushu',
    ),

