    CultureContent(
      id: 'ukiyo_e_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IonheGyjG-s',
      categoryId: 'art',
      title: 'Ukiyo-e: Art of the Floating World',
      subtitle: 'Woodblock prints that captured everyday beauty',
      description: '''Ukiyo-e means "pictures of the floating world"—an art form capturing the beauty of everyday life, entertainment, and landscapes in Edo-period Japan (1603-1867).

Famous artists:
- **Hokusai**: Dramatic landscapes like "The Great Wave off Kanagawa," one of the world's most recognized artworks. He created 30,000 works in his lifetime and was still producing masterpieces at 88
- **Hiroshige**: Master of atmospheric landscape and seasonal mood—his "One Hundred Famous Views of Edo" influenced Van Gogh, who copied several prints in oil paint
- **Utamaro**: Captured the beauty of women (bijin-ga) with psychological depth; portrayed them as people, not objects
- **Sharaku**: Mysterious artist who produced 145 kabuki actor portraits in 10 months then vanished

Ukiyo-e democratized art—prints could be produced in editions and sold for the price of a bowl of noodles. The common people of Edo decorated their homes with beauty.

The printing process was collaborative: the artist designed, woodcarvers cut (sometimes requiring 20+ blocks for one image), and printers carefully registered each color.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1578301978162-7aae4d755dd5?w=800',
      tags: ['ukiyo-e', 'woodblock', 'hokusai', 'hiroshige', 'art history'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 14),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ukiyo-e',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'wabi_sabi_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5lyo3LE7FwE',
      categoryId: 'art',
      title: 'Wabi-Sabi: The Aesthetics of Imperfection',
      subtitle: 'Finding beauty in the broken, worn, and incomplete',
      description: '''Wabi-sabi is perhaps Japan's most profound aesthetic philosophy—a worldview centered on the acceptance and celebration of transience, imperfection, and incompleteness. It is the opposite of the Western pursuit of perfection and permanence.

The two components:
- **Wabi**: The beauty of asymmetry, simplicity, and restraint. Originally connoted the quiet desolation of living in nature, but evolved to mean finding contentment in simplicity
- **Sabi**: The beauty of time passing—the weathered, the rusted, the aged. A cracked tea bowl repaired with gold (kintsugi) is more beautiful for having been broken

Wabi-sabi appears everywhere in Japanese culture:
- **Tea ceremony**: Rough, irregular tea bowls are valued over perfectly symmetrical porcelain
- **Gardens**: Raked gravel in rock gardens is never perfectly smooth; moss is allowed to grow unevenly
- **Architecture**: Exposed wood, aged beams, and natural materials showing use
- **Ikebana**: Bent, asymmetrical branches chosen over perfectly straight ones

The three marks of wabi-sabi:
1. **Nothing is perfect**: The crack, the asymmetry, the fade
2. **Nothing is complete**: The garden always in process
3. **Nothing is permanent**: Embrace that all things pass

Kintsugi—the art of repairing broken pottery with gold lacquer—is wabi-sabi made visible. A bowl broken and repaired this way has a history; it is more interesting, more real, than one that was never broken.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1524413840807-0c3cb6fa808d?w=800',
      tags: ['wabi-sabi', 'aesthetics', 'philosophy', 'kintsugi', 'beauty'],
      level: 3,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 13),
      readTime: 7,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wabi-sabi',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'japanese_garden_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UyUmw3q3sGU',
      categoryId: 'art',
      title: 'Japanese Gardens: A Philosophical Landscape',
      subtitle: 'Every stone and stream placed with intention',
      description: '''A Japanese garden is not merely decorative—it is a representation of the universe, built according to precise philosophical principles. Walking through one is a meditation.

Major types:
- **Karesansui (dry landscape / rock garden)**: Gravel raked to represent water; rocks representing mountains, islands, or crouching tigers. The most famous is Ryoan-ji in Kyoto—15 rocks arranged so that from the veranda where visitors traditionally sit, one rock is always hidden from view
- **Tsukiyama (hill garden)**: Designed to evoke mountains and valleys, often with a central pond representing the sea
- **Roji (tea garden)**: The path to the tea house, designed to create mental transition from ordinary life to the sacred tea space
- **Stroll garden (kaiyushiki)**: Large gardens revealing different views as you walk—designed to be experienced in motion

Core design principles:
- **Shakkei (borrowed scenery)**: Incorporating the surrounding landscape (mountains, trees) as a backdrop, extending the garden beyond its walls
- **Ma (negative space)**: Empty gravel and silent stone are as important as the garden elements
- **Miniaturization**: A small stone can represent a mountain; a shallow pool represents the ocean
- **Hide-and-reveal**: Curved paths and strategically placed stones conceal what lies ahead

Great Japanese gardens: Kenroku-en (Kanazawa), Koraku-en (Okayama), Kairaku-en (Mito), Shinjuku Gyoen (Tokyo).''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1492571350019-22de08371fd3?w=800',
      tags: ['garden', 'design', 'landscape', 'philosophy', 'karesansui'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 12),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_garden',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'origami_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LkwtUZKKYNM',
      categoryId: 'art',
      title: 'Origami: The Art of Paper Transformation',
      subtitle: 'From sacred ritual to mathematical art form',
      description: '''Origami (from "oru" = fold, "kami" = paper) began in Japan as a ceremonial practice—formal paper folding was used in Shinto ceremonies and gift-wrapping (noshi). The tradition of folding the iconic crane (orizuru) as a symbol of peace originated from the belief that anyone who folds 1,000 cranes (senbazuru) will be granted a wish.

The art form has several levels:
- **Traditional**: Classic models passed down through generations—the crane, the fox, the frog (which can jump when its back is pressed)
- **Modular origami**: Multiple identical units folded and assembled into complex 3D structures
- **Kirigami**: Paper folding combined with cutting
- **Wet-folding**: Dampening paper to allow gentle curves—used for organic, sculptural forms

Modern origami became a mathematical discipline when Japanese mathematician Humiaki Huzita developed axioms describing all possible origami folds. These have real-world applications:
- Airbag deployment patterns (how to pack a large surface in a small space)
- Satellite solar panel deployment in space
- Medical stents that open from a compressed state
- Architectural folding structures

The "reverse fold" and "inside reverse fold" are fundamental moves that take years to execute perfectly. Master origami artists like Akira Yoshizawa (1911–2005) elevated the art form to international recognition — Yoshizawa received the Order of the Rising Sun from the Emperor in 1983 for his lifelong contribution.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1558618666-fcd25c85cd64?w=800',
      tags: ['origami', 'paper', 'folding', 'crane', 'mathematics'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 11),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Origami',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'manga_anime_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HgLPRrmiShc',
      categoryId: 'art',
      title: 'Manga & Anime: Japan\'s Global Visual Language',
      subtitle: 'How comic art became a worldwide cultural export',
      description: '''Manga (Japanese comics) and anime (Japanese animation) are Japan's most globally recognized cultural exports—consumed by hundreds of millions worldwide and generating billions in revenue annually. But they are deeply rooted in Japanese tradition.

Historical roots:
- Manga's visual style descends from 12th-century animal caricatures (choju-giga), ukiyo-e woodblock prints, and imported American comics
- Osamu Tezuka ("God of Manga") created the fundamental visual language in the 1950s, drawing inspiration from Disney but developing something distinctly Japanese
- The large eyes in manga/anime were influenced by early Disney animation; their expressive power became a Japanese artistic convention

Key characteristics of manga art:
- **Reading direction**: Right to left (following Japanese writing direction)—many foreign readers find this confusing at first but natural after adjustment
- **Speed lines**: Dynamic diagonal lines creating sense of motion
- **Sweat drops, vein marks, and chibi forms**: Emotion shorthand understood globally
- **Genres for every audience**: Shonen (boys), shojo (girls), seinen (adult men), josei (adult women), plus countless subgenres

Anime is distinct from Western animation:
- Often adapted from manga; story arcs can span years
- Limited animation style allows complex stories with smaller budgets
- Studio Ghibli (Miyazaki) raised the art form to worldwide critical recognition

Akihabara in Tokyo is the center of otaku (manga/anime enthusiast) culture, though shops, conventions (comiket), and cafes are throughout Japan.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1607604276583-eef5d076aa5f?w=800',
      tags: ['manga', 'anime', 'pop culture', 'ghibli', 'tezuka'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 10),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manga',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'japanese_architecture_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zvoUMkgHph4',
      categoryId: 'art',
      title: 'Japanese Architecture: The Art of Lightness',
      subtitle: 'Wood, paper, and the philosophy of flexible space',
      description: '''Traditional Japanese architecture represents a fundamentally different approach to building—one that embraces the natural environment, creates flexible interior space, and expresses profound philosophical ideas.

Core principles:
- **Wood as primary material**: Unlike European stone buildings meant to last forever, traditional Japanese buildings are designed to be repaired and replaced. Ise Grand Shrine is completely rebuilt every 20 years using the same techniques for 1,300+ years—this is a living building
- **Shoji screens**: Paper-and-wood sliding panels replace solid walls. They filter light beautifully and allow space to be reconfigured
- **Engawa**: The transitional veranda between interior and garden—neither inside nor outside—a liminal space for contemplating the garden
- **Post-and-beam construction**: Allows walls to be non-structural; earthquakes shake the flexible frame rather than cracking rigid walls

The Japanese room (washitsu):
- **Tatami**: Woven rush mats defining the floor, also used to measure room size. The precise scent and texture underfoot is a distinct sensory experience
- **Tokonoma**: An alcove for displaying art—a seasonal scroll and single flower arrangement. Everything else is removed for this one focal point
- **Fusuma**: Painted sliding room dividers, often depicting landscapes

Modern Japanese architecture (Tadao Ando, Kengo Kuma, Toyo Ito) extends traditional principles into concrete and steel—love of natural light, integration with landscape, and simplicity.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1624451579834-e0a46f8c56de?w=800',
      tags: ['architecture', 'wood', 'tatami', 'shoji', 'design'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 9),
      readTime: 7,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_architecture',
    ),

