    CultureContent(
      id: 'kanji_origins_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bPU-pRZr0EU',
      categoryId: 'language',
      title: 'Kanji: Written Stories',
      subtitle: 'How Chinese characters became Japanese',
      description: '''Kanji are Chinese characters adapted for Japanese, and each one tells a story. Understanding kanji origins reveals how Japanese culture absorbed Chinese influence while creating something uniquely its own.

Examples of etymology:
- **木 (ki/tree)**: A simple picture of a tree
- **森 (mori/forest)**: Three trees together = forest
- **休 (yasu/rest)**: Person leaning against a tree = rest
- **明 (aka/bright)**: Sun + moon together = bright
- **愛 (ai/love)**: Contains a heart (心) in the center, and a catching/walking motion—love that catches and follows

Learning kanji is a lifelong journey. Japanese children learn about 2,136 "jōyō kanji" (common characters) by the end of high school. Each character often contains a radical (bushu)—a component that hints at meaning. For example, characters related to water contain the radical 水; characters for emotions often contain 心 (heart).

Stroke order matters deeply—there is a correct sequence to draw each character, developed over centuries to flow naturally from top to bottom, left to right. Wrong stroke order produces characters that "look wrong" to trained eyes.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1605407867590-40eb5a01eeac?w=800',
      tags: ['kanji', 'language', 'writing', 'characters', 'etymology'],
