    CultureContent(
      id: 'keigo_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SnmEXprKUqs',
      categoryId: 'language',
      title: 'Keigo: The Language of Respect',
      subtitle: 'How Japanese politeness is built into grammar itself',
      description: '''Japanese has an elaborate system of respectful speech called keigo—arguably the world's most grammatically sophisticated politeness system. Social relationships are literally embedded in verb forms, not just word choice.

The three levels of keigo:
- **Teineigo (polite language)**: Standard politeness using -masu and -desu forms. What all Japanese learn first and use in professional settings
- **Sonkeigo (respectful language)**: Used when speaking TO or ABOUT someone of higher status. Special verb forms: iku (go) → irassharu; iru (be) → irassharu; suru (do) → nasaru
- **Kenjōgo (humble language)**: Used to lower yourself when speaking about your own actions to someone of higher status. iku (go) → mairu; iru (be) → oru; suru (do) → itasu

Keigo in business is demanding—Japanese companies spend significant time training employees in proper keigo for customer service. Mistakes can damage business relationships.

The same action expressed in three levels:
- Casual: 食べる (taberu) — eat
- Polite: 食べます (tabemasu) — eat (politely)
- Respectful: 召し上がる (meshiagaru) — [honored person] eats
- Humble: いただく (itadaku) — [I] humbly eat

"Itadakimasu" before meals uses the humble form—you humbly receive this food from all who made it possible.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1607604276583-eef5d076aa5f?w=800',
      tags: ['keigo', 'language', 'politeness', 'grammar', 'social'],
      level: 3,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 13),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honorific_speech_in_Japanese',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'japanese_sounds_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kMxGkXYsjyM',
      categoryId: 'language',
      title: 'Onomatopoeia: Japanese\'s Secret Layer',
      subtitle: '4,500 sound words that paint pictures with language',
      description: '''Japanese has approximately 4,500 onomatopoeia—vastly more than English or any European language. These words don't just describe sounds; they describe textures, emotions, states of being, and sensations with extraordinary precision.

Types of Japanese sound symbolism:
- **Giongo**: Actual sounds → pika-pika (sparkling), goro-goro (rumbling)
- **Giseigo**: Animal and human sounds → wan-wan (dog bark), nyan-nyan (cat meow)
- **Gitaigo**: States and feelings → fura-fura (dizzy/unsteady), fuwa-fuwa (soft/fluffy/dreamy), niko-niko (smiling), baka-baka (stupid actions)
- **Giyougo**: Movement and actions → suro-suro (smoothly), noro-noro (slowly/sluggishly)

Examples that have no English equivalent:
- **Nuru-nuru**: Slippery in a wet, organic way (like eel or seaweed)
- **Zawa-zawa**: The unsettled feeling when something is wrong—a rustling unease
- **Hiya-hiya**: The specific anxiety of watching someone do something dangerous
- **Shiku-shiku**: Quiet, continuous sobbing
- **Kira-kira**: Sparkling, glittering, often used for talented people

Manga and anime use these words visually—they appear as text in action scenes, making the reading experience more immersive. Pikachu's name comes from "pika-pika" (sparkle) + "chuu" (mouse sound).''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1558618666-fcd25c85cd64?w=800',
      tags: ['onomatopoeia', 'language', 'sounds', 'expression', 'unique'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 12),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_sound_symbolism',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'calligraphy_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xD9l9TaTTSQ',
      categoryId: 'language',
      title: 'Shodo: The Way of Writing',
      subtitle: 'Calligraphy as meditation, art, and self-expression',
      description: '''Shodo (calligraphy, literally "the way of writing") is one of Japan's most practiced traditional arts—children study it in school, and adults pursue it as lifelong practice and meditation.

The tools are called the "Four Treasures of the Study":
- **Fude (brush)**: Made from animal hair—wolf, rabbit, horse—each producing different strokes
- **Sumi (ink)**: Made from soot mixed with animal glue; grinding the ink stick against a wet inkstone is itself meditative, preparing the mind
- **Suzuri (inkstone)**: Flat stone with a well for water; grinding produces ink of the desired consistency
- **Washi (paper)**: Traditional handmade paper; different papers absorb ink differently

Scripts used in shodo:
- **Kaisho**: Standard block script—clear, structured, easy to read
- **Gyosho**: Semi-cursive—flowing but legible, used in everyday calligraphy
- **Sosho**: Full cursive—extremely abbreviated strokes, requires years of study to read
- **Tensho**: Seal script—ancient form used for hanko (personal seals)

The master calligrapher's brushstroke shows the painter's state of mind at the moment of creation—hesitation, force, serenity, and speed all appear in the stroke quality. A single character can take years to master.

New Year calligraphy (kakizome) on January 2nd is a beloved family tradition.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1605407867590-40eb5a01eeac?w=800',
      tags: ['shodo', 'calligraphy', 'writing', 'meditation', 'brush'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 11),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_calligraphy',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'haiku_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SBo2Oii6-Y8',
      categoryId: 'language',
      title: 'Haiku: The World in Seventeen Syllables',
      subtitle: 'A flash of perception that stops time',
      description: '''Haiku is the world's shortest literary form—17 syllables in a 5-7-5 pattern—yet within it, a master can capture an entire world, a season, a feeling, or a moment of enlightenment.

The rules of classical haiku:
- **Kigo (seasonal word)**: Every haiku must contain a word that indicates the season. "Cherry blossom" = spring; "cicada" = summer; "red leaf" = autumn; "snow" = winter
- **Kireji (cutting word)**: A grammatical pause that divides the haiku into two juxtaposed images, creating a spark between them
- **Present-tense observation**: Haiku captures a moment of direct perception, not memory or reflection

The three haiku masters:
- **Matsuo Basho**: "The old pond—/ a frog jumps in,/ sound of water." Basho elevated haiku from wordplay to spiritual practice; he walked thousands of miles seeking poetic enlightenment
- **Yosa Buson**: A painter who brought visual intensity to haiku—his poems feel like paintings
- **Kobayashi Issa**: Known for humanity and humor—"This world of dew/ is only a world of dew—/ and yet... and yet..."

Modern haiku movements exist worldwide. The formal rules are debated but the core—a moment of perception, usually in nature, with a surprising juxtaposition—remains.

The practice of "haiku walk" (haiku walk in the moment, not writing about the past) is itself meditative.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1492571350019-22de08371fd3?w=800',
      tags: ['haiku', 'poetry', 'literature', 'basho', 'seasonal'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 10),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haiku',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'japanese_writing_systems_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r7a8OjvViwE',
      categoryId: 'language',
      title: 'The Three Writing Systems of Japanese',
      subtitle: 'Why Japanese uses three alphabets simultaneously',
      description: '''Japanese uses three writing systems simultaneously—often mixing all three in a single sentence. This seems confusing until you understand what each system does.

**Hiragana (ひらがな)**:
- 46 characters, each representing a syllable sound
- Used for: grammatical particles, verb endings, native Japanese words
- History: Derived by simplifying kanji in the 9th century, created primarily by women (aristocratic women wrote literature in hiragana while men used Chinese)
- Feel: Soft, rounded, flowing

**Katakana (カタカナ)**:
- Also 46 characters for the same sounds
- Used for: foreign loanwords (computer, television, coffee), foreign names, scientific terms, emphasis (like italics), robot/computer speech in manga
- History: Derived from parts of kanji, developed by Buddhist monks
- Feel: Sharp, angular, "foreign"

**Kanji (漢字)**:
- Chinese-derived ideographs; modern Japanese uses ~2,000 in daily life
- Used for: nouns, verb stems, adjectives
- Feel: Dense with meaning

A sample sentence: 私はコーヒーを飲みます (I drink coffee)
- 私 (watashi) = kanji for "I"
- は (wa) = hiragana particle
- コーヒー (ko-hi) = katakana for "coffee" (foreign word)
- を (wo) = hiragana particle
- 飲みます (nomimasu) = kanji 飲 + hiragana みます

This mixing is natural and automatic for Japanese speakers—the visual variety actually makes text easier to parse at a glance.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1607604276583-eef5d076aa5f?w=800',
      tags: ['hiragana', 'katakana', 'kanji', 'writing systems', 'language'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 9),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_writing_system',
    ),

