    CultureContent(
      id: 'kintsugi_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AHf-rGrh3c4',
      isPremium: true,
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Kintsugi: The Art of Precious Scars',
      subtitle: 'Repairing broken pottery with gold to make it more beautiful',
      description: '''Kintsugi ("golden joinery") is the 500-year-old Japanese art of repairing broken ceramics with lacquer mixed with gold, silver, or platinum powder. Rather than hiding the damage, kintsugi highlights it—turning the fracture lines into golden rivers that make the object more beautiful than before.

The philosophy behind kintsugi is profound:
- A broken and repaired bowl has a history. That history is its value.
- Imperfection and damage are part of the object's journey, not flaws to be ashamed of
- This directly embodies wabi-sabi (beauty in imperfection) and mottainai (nothing should go to waste)

The process:
1. Collect all broken pieces, no matter how small
2. Apply urushi lacquer (from the urushi tree) to bind the pieces
3. Dust the wet lacquer with gold powder before it dries
4. Sand and polish through multiple layers over weeks or months
5. The final surface is durable—more so than the original

Kintsugi has been extended as a metaphor for human resilience. Psychologists use it to help people reframe their wounds and scars as marks of strength rather than weakness. The idea: "You are not less for having been broken. You are more."

Workshops in Kyoto and Tokyo teach kintsugi with real urushi lacquer. Short "kintsugi experience" classes using quick-drying compounds are also popular.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1578301978162-7aae4d755dd5?w=800',
      tags: ['kintsugi', 'ceramics', 'wabi-sabi', 'repair', 'gold'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 8),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kintsugi',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'matsuri_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CnLU5Vd0N2E',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Matsuri: The Japanese Festival',
      subtitle: 'How communities celebrate with gods, fire, and portable shrines',
      description: '''Japan has over 300,000 matsuri (festivals) each year—nearly one for every shrine. Each festival honors a local kami (spirit) and expresses the unique character of its community.

The heart of most matsuri is the mikoshi—a portable shrine that temporarily houses the kami. Teams of festival-goers (often wearing happi coats and headbands) carry the mikoshi through the streets on their shoulders, chanting "wasshoi! wasshoi!" The energetic motion is believed to excite the kami and spread divine blessing.

Famous matsuri:
- **Awa Odori** (Tokushima, August): 400-year-old dance festival where over 1 million people dance through the streets. "Those who dance are fools; those who watch are also fools—if we're both fools, we might as well dance!"
- **Gion Matsuri** (Kyoto, July): UNESCO-listed festival featuring floats (yamaboko) up to 25 meters tall paraded through Kyoto, dating back 1,150 years
- **Nebuta Matsuri** (Aomori, August): Enormous illuminated warrior floats parade through the city at night
- **Tenjin Matsuri** (Osaka, July): River procession with over 100 boats and fireworks

Food stalls (yatai) are inseparable from matsuri: takoyaki (octopus balls), yakisoba noodles, kakigori (shaved ice), chocolate banana, and goldfish scooping for children.

The festival's sacred and festive elements are deliberately intertwined—the celebration IS the worship.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1590559899731-a382839e5549?w=800',
      tags: ['matsuri', 'festival', 'shrine', 'mikoshi', 'celebration'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 7),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matsuri',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'sado_wagashi_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oK1wEGcWG3c',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'The Art of Gift-Giving in Japan',
      subtitle: 'Omiyage, noshi, and the ritual of thoughtful presentation',
      description: '''Gift-giving in Japan is an art with elaborate codes, seasonal timing, and profound social meaning. The presentation often matters as much as the gift itself.

Key gift-giving traditions:
- **Omiyage**: Souvenirs brought back from travel, given to neighbors, coworkers, and family. Not bringing omiyage after a trip is considered rude. Japanese train stations and airports have extensive omiyage sections with region-specific sweets, snacks, and crafts
- **Ochugen** (mid-July): Summer gift-giving season, typically food items sent to people who have helped you
- **Oseibo** (December): Year-end gift-giving season, expressing gratitude for the year's kindness

Gift presentation rules:
- Always wrap gifts beautifully—the wrapping paper and ribbon should be as thoughtful as the gift
- Use both hands when giving and receiving gifts; bow slightly
- Gifts are traditionally NOT opened in front of the giver (to avoid any visible disappointment)
- Odd numbers are auspicious; even numbers can suggest "splitting" relationships

Noshi (a traditional bow made from dried abalone) is the origin of the decorative tape seen on gift packaging. The thin strips that decorate Japanese gift wrapping paper recall this ancient symbol of an auspicious gift.

Money gifts (in envelopes called "shugi-bukuro") for weddings must be in new, uncreased bills—old bills suggest you prepared in advance for someone's misfortune.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1558618666-fcd25c85cd64?w=800',
      tags: ['gifts', 'omiyage', 'etiquette', 'social customs', 'presentation'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 6),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gifts_in_Japanese_culture',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'traditional_clothing_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xtVsJadqFhk',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Kimono: Japan\'s Living Garment',
      subtitle: 'A thousand years of textile art still worn today',
      description: '''The kimono ("thing to wear") is Japan's traditional garment, still worn at festivals, ceremonies, and in daily life by those who practice traditional arts. It is one of the world's most sophisticated clothing systems.

Types of kimono by formality:
- **Furisode**: Long-sleeved kimono for unmarried women; the most formal and colorful
- **Tomesode**: Short-sleeved with patterns only below the waist; worn by married women at formal events
- **Yukata**: Cotton summer kimono; casual, often worn at festivals and onsen towns
- **Hakama**: Wide-legged divided skirts worn over kimono for martial arts, ceremonies, and university graduation

The textile art of kimono:
- **Nishiki-ori**: Brocade weaving using multiple threads to create intricate patterns
- **Yuzen**: Hand-painted resist dyeing, developed in Kyoto; complex pictorial designs
- **Shibori**: Tie-dye technique creating patterns through binding, stitching, and clamping
- **Indigo (ai)**: Deep blue from natural plant dye, requiring multiple immersions to build depth

Wearing a kimono correctly requires training—the layers, obi (wide sash), accessories, and geta (wooden sandals) form a complete system. A skilled dresser (kitsuke) can help you wear one properly in 20-30 minutes.

Kimono rental shops in Kyoto, Asakusa (Tokyo), and Kanazawa allow visitors to wear kimono while exploring historical districts.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1624451579834-e0a46f8c56de?w=800',
      tags: ['kimono', 'clothing', 'textile', 'tradition', 'fashion'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 5),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kimono',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'bushido_samurai_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=35UfydQgBm8',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'The Samurai and Bushido',
      subtitle: 'The warrior code that shaped Japanese values',
      description: '''The samurai (warrior class) dominated Japan for nearly 700 years (1185-1868) and left an indelible mark on Japanese culture, values, and aesthetics. Their code of conduct, bushido ("the way of the warrior"), continues to influence Japanese society.

Bushido's core virtues:
- **Gi (義 righteousness)**: Acting with moral integrity even when no one is watching
- **Yu (勇 courage)**: Heroic courage—not reckless, but principled
- **Jin (仁 benevolence)**: Compassion and mercy toward others
- **Rei (礼 respect)**: Courtesy and right action
- **Makoto (誠 honesty)**: Total sincerity, with no deception
- **Meiyo (名誉 honor)**: Living and dying with honor
- **Chugi (忠義 loyalty)**: Absolute loyalty to one's master

Samurai culture produced many of Japan's defining arts:
- Bonsai (originally a warrior's meditation practice)
- Noh theater (developed under samurai patronage)
- Japanese garden design
- Calligraphy (a samurai's daily practice)
- The tea ceremony (Zen practice before battle)

The concept of seppuku (ritual suicide to preserve honor) reflects the extreme expression of these values. More commonly, the samurai's relationship with death produced "living fully"—every action taken as if it might be your last.

The Meiji Restoration (1868) abolished the samurai class, but their values were deliberately reintroduced in the 20th century to inspire patriotism—with tragic results in WWII. Modern Japan wrestles with how to honor samurai heritage without nationalism.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1599072200154-abc0dbf65cbb?w=800',
      tags: ['samurai', 'bushido', 'warrior', 'history', 'values'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 4),
      readTime: 7,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samurai',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'anime_otaku_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HgLPRrmiShc',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Otaku Culture: From Subculture to Superculture',
      subtitle: 'How Japan\'s fan culture conquered the world',
      description: '''The word "otaku" originally meant "you" (a formal second-person pronoun) but evolved into a term for obsessive fans, first of anime and manga, then of any niche. Once a social stigma, "otaku" is now embraced globally—and Japan's Creative Economy generates over \$20 billion annually from it.

What makes Japanese fan culture distinctive:
- **Doujinshi**: Fan-created comics based on existing properties, sold at Comiket (the world's largest self-publishing event, 750,000 attendees twice yearly). Many professional manga-ka began as doujinshi artists
- **Cosplay**: "Costume play"—fans create and wear elaborate costumes of fictional characters. Japan arguably invented modern cosplay culture; its craftsmanship standards are extraordinary
- **Idol culture**: Carefully managed pop groups (AKB48, Johnny's) where fans purchase multiple copies of the same CD for voting rights; attend handshake events; follow detailed media portrayals of the "members' daily lives"
- **Gacha games**: Randomized digital prize machines in smartphone games; the monetization model that made Japan the world's most profitable mobile gaming market

Areas where otaku culture concentrates:
- **Akihabara** (Tokyo): Electronics + anime; the "sacred site" of otaku culture
- **Den Den Town** (Osaka): Akihabara equivalent in Kansai
- **Nakano Broadway** (Tokyo): Dense concentration of rare collectibles

The global spread: anime is now watched in every country; Japanese games dominate worldwide; manga translation is mainstream in bookstores everywhere. "Otaku" has been fully reclaimed as a positive identity.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1607604276583-eef5d076aa5f?w=800',
      tags: ['otaku', 'anime', 'manga', 'fan culture', 'akihabara'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 3),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otaku',
    ),

