    CultureContent(
      id: 'izakaya_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cv4YMWP7T5w',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Izakaya: Japan\'s Pub Culture',
      subtitle: 'Where after-work drinking becomes a social institution',
      description: '''The izakaya ("stay-drink-place") is Japan's answer to the pub, tapas bar, and restaurant simultaneously. It is where Japanese office workers decompress, friendships are cemented, and business relationships actually formed—more real conversation happens here than in any boardroom.

Izakaya culture and etiquette:
- **Otoshi**: A small appetizer brought automatically when you sit—you didn't order it, but you pay for it. It is the cover charge disguised as food
- **Nomunication**: A Japanese portmanteau of "nomu" (drink) and "communication"—the understanding that alcohol creates the social lubrication for honest conversation that formal Japanese culture otherwise suppresses
- **Kanpai**: "Cheers!" Always wait for everyone to have a drink before raising glasses. The first drink is typically beer; subsequent rounds can be anything
- **Never pour your own drink**: Pour for others; they will pour for you. This continuous service is the visible form of mutual care

Classic izakaya food:
- **Edamame**: Salted boiled soybeans—eaten while deciding what else to order
- **Karaage**: Twice-fried chicken in soy-ginger marinade; crispy outside, juicy inside
- **Yakitori**: Skewered chicken parts grilled over charcoal—every part of the chicken used with different seasoning
- **Dashimaki tamago**: Rolled egg omelette with dashi broth inside
- **Takowasa**: Raw octopus in wasabi sauce—an acquired taste but addictive

Drinks: draft beer, highball (whisky and soda—extremely popular), sake, shochu, and cocktails.

Chains like Watami and Torikizoku offer reliable quality at low prices. Standing izakaya (tachinomi) are even cheaper and more casual.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1600880292203-757bb62b4baf?w=800',
      tags: ['izakaya', 'drinking', 'social', 'yakitori', 'pub culture'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 8),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izakaya',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'japanese_convenience_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mKg4aZvKVu0',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Conbini: The Japanese Convenience Store Revolution',
      subtitle: 'Why 7-Eleven in Japan is nothing like 7-Eleven anywhere else',
      description: '''Japan's convenience stores (conbini) are an economic and culinary miracle—over 56,000 stores nationwide, open 24/7, selling fresh food that rivals many restaurants in quality and variety.

Why Japanese conbini are different:
- **Onigiri**: Fresh rice balls with fillings—tuna mayo, salmon, pickled plum, kombu—made fresh multiple times daily. The three-step opening package keeps the nori crispy until you want it
- **Hot food counter**: Nikuman (steamed pork buns), corn dogs, fried chicken, and seasonal items cooked fresh throughout the day. The smells when you enter are entirely intentional
- **Prepared meals**: Bento boxes, yakisoba, pasta—all heated by the store staff ("atatakakunasimasu ka?" = "shall I warm that?")
- **Desserts**: Pudding, parfaits, roll cakes, and seasonal limited editions that generate genuine social media excitement

Conbini also function as:
- ATMs (one of the few places to find ATMs that work with foreign cards)
- Ticket printing (concerts, movies, theme parks)
- Tax payment and bill payment
- Package shipping and receiving (takuhaibin)
- Printing and fax
- Coffee (surprisingly excellent drip coffee for ¥100)

The three main chains are 7-Eleven, FamilyMart, and Lawson. Each has loyal fans who debate seasonal limited editions with genuine passion. Seasonal items (sakura-flavored in spring, sweet potato in autumn) are a cultural phenomenon.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1569758269042-a9e3b38be36e?w=800',
      tags: ['convenience store', 'conbini', 'onigiri', 'food culture', 'daily life'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 7),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convenience_store_in_Japan',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'japanese_breakfast_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRIDcCLxdRI',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Asa-gohan: The Japanese Breakfast',
      subtitle: 'Why Japan starts the day with miso soup and rice',
      description: '''The traditional Japanese breakfast (asa-gohan, literally "morning rice") is a complete meal built around a different logic than Western breakfast. Rather than sweet foods to start the day, it begins with umami—the savory, satisfying fifth taste.

A classic Japanese breakfast:
- **Gohan (steamed rice)**: The anchor of every traditional meal, including breakfast
- **Miso shiru (miso soup)**: Dashi broth with seasonal vegetables, tofu, wakame seaweed—the single most comforting food in Japan
- **Yakizakana (grilled fish)**: Often salmon, mackerel, or dried horse mackerel (aji no hiraki)
- **Tamagoyaki**: Sweetened rolled egg omelette
- **Tsukemono (pickles)**: Fermented vegetables that aid digestion and add acidity to balance the meal
- **Natto**: Fermented soybeans with a sticky, stringy texture and powerful smell—divisive even among Japanese. High in vitamin K2 and protein; eaten with raw egg yolk and soy sauce over rice

The Japanese breakfast was recognized by researchers studying centenarians—the combination of fermented foods (miso, natto, pickles), fish (omega-3s), vegetables, and minimal sugar may contribute to Japan's exceptional longevity.

Hotel breakfasts in Japan often offer "Japanese or Western" choice. The Japanese option is always the more interesting meal—the Western version is a pale imitation.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1547592180-85f173990554?w=800',
      tags: ['breakfast', 'miso soup', 'natto', 'rice', 'morning'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 6),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_cuisine',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'wagyu_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R2hYj0prPaQ',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Wagyu: The World\'s Most Extraordinary Beef',
      subtitle: 'Marbling, genetics, and a 150-year breeding story',
      description: '''Wagyu (literally "Japanese cow") is the collective name for four Japanese cattle breeds raised using methods that produce extraordinary marbling—fat distributed through muscle tissue in a snowflake-like pattern that creates butter-like texture and intense flavor.

The genetics:
- Japanese Black (Kuroge Washu): Produces the famous Kobe, Matsusaka, and Omi beef. The marbling (shimofuri) appears in individual muscle fibers
- Japanese Brown: Leaner, grassier flavor profile
- Japanese Shorthorn: Rarer, more robust flavor
- Japanese Polled: Extremely rare, small population

Regional branded wagyu:
- **Kobe Beef**: From Tajima-strain Japanese Black cattle raised in Hyogo Prefecture. To be certified "Kobe Beef," cattle must score at least A4 or B4 on the grading system and achieve BMS (Beef Marbling Score) of 6-12
- **Matsusaka Beef**: Often considered superior to Kobe by connoisseurs; only heifers, fed on beer and massaged
- **Omi Beef**: Japan's oldest branded beef, from Shiga Prefecture

The marbling produces a low melting point—wagyu fat melts at body temperature, literally dissolving on the tongue. The umami comes from oleic acid, glutamate, and inosinic acid in higher concentrations than other beef.

Cooking wagyu: thinly sliced for shabu-shabu (hot pot) or sukiyaki, or in small portions for yakiniku (grilled). A proper steak portion is 150-200g—the richness means less is more.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1579584425555-c3ce17fd4351?w=800',
      tags: ['wagyu', 'beef', 'kobe', 'marbling', 'gourmet'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 5),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wagyu',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'japanese_fermentation_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F5m7Lr8y0jA',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Koji: Japan\'s Magic Mold',
      subtitle: 'The microorganism that created sake, miso, soy sauce, and mirin',
      description: '''Koji (Aspergillus oryzae) is a mold that transforms simple ingredients into some of the world's most complex flavors. Without koji, there would be no sake, no miso, no soy sauce, no mirin, no rice vinegar—essentially no Japanese cuisine as we know it.

How koji works:
- Koji grows on steamed rice, barley, or soybeans in a warm, humid environment for 40-50 hours
- During this time, it produces enzymes (amylases and proteases) that break down starches and proteins into sugars and amino acids
- These sugars can then ferment into alcohol (sake, mirin) or combine with salt to create umami-rich pastes (miso, soy sauce)

Koji's major applications:
- **Shio koji** (salt koji): A paste of koji + salt used as a dry rub for fish, chicken, and vegetables. The enzymes tenderize the protein and produce umami over 24-48 hours of marinating
- **Amazake**: Sweet fermented rice drink made with koji but no alcohol. Often sold at shrines in winter
- **Mirin**: Sweet cooking sake; made by fermenting koji with glutinous rice and shochu
- **Doburoku**: Unfiltered rice wine made with koji—technically illegal for home production in Japan

The global food world has embraced koji: chefs at Noma (Copenhagen), Eleven Madison Park (New York), and other top restaurants use it to cure meats, age steaks, and add depth to vegetables. The New York Times called it "the secret ingredient behind Japan's greatest foods."''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1600880292203-757bb62b4baf?w=800',
      tags: ['koji', 'fermentation', 'miso', 'sake', 'umami'],
      level: 3,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 4),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aspergillus_oryzae',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'japanese_sweets_shop_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2rvvC1kkkVs',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Dagashi: The Childhood Sweet Shop',
      subtitle: 'Cheap, nostalgic candies and the culture of shared joy',
      description: '''Dagashi ("cheap sweets") are inexpensive Japanese candies and snacks sold for 10-100 yen each, designed specifically for children with very small allowances. They are not just snacks—they are a social institution.

Famous dagashi and their mechanics:
- **Umaibo**: Puffed corn sticks in 20 flavors (takoyaki, salami, mentaiko); each costs exactly 10 yen since 1979
- **Ramune candy**: Fizzy carbonated candy tablets in the iconic bottle with a marble stopper; the game of getting the marble out is part of the experience
- **Kabayaki-san Taro**: Dried fish snack shaped like an eel, eaten with "Kinako Boy" corn puff in a famous flavor pairing
- **Bikkuri Man**: Chocolate wafers with holographic sticker cards inside; the 1980s craze was so intense children were throwing away the wafers to get the stickers

Dagashi shops (dagashiya) were the original 10-yen stores—tiny shops stuffed floor to ceiling with colorful packages, where children debated how to spend their 50-yen allowance. Modern dagashi cafes and themed shops preserve this nostalgia.

The manga/anime "Dagashi Kashi" popularized dagashi culture internationally, with one episode per dagashi explaining its history and how to eat it. A new generation of international fans now order dagashi sets online.

Dagashi culture reflects an important Japanese value: maximum joy from minimum resources.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1558618666-fcd25c85cd64?w=800',
      tags: ['dagashi', 'candy', 'childhood', 'nostalgia', 'snacks'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 3),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dagashi',
    ),

