    CultureContent(
      id: 'japanese_proverbs_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5lyo3LE7FwE',
      categoryId: 'language',
      title: 'Kotowaza: Japanese Proverbs and Their Wisdom',
      subtitle: 'Ancient sayings that reveal how Japan thinks',
      description: '''Kotowaza (proverbs) are concentrated cultural wisdom—short sayings that reveal what a society values, fears, and believes about human nature. Japanese proverbs offer a window into a distinctly different worldview.

Proverbs that reveal Japanese values:

**On patience and persistence:**
- 七転び八起き (Nana korobi ya oki): "Fall seven times, stand up eight." The number of times you rise is always one more than the falls.
- 石の上にも三年 (Ishi no ue ni mo sannen): "Even a stone warms after sitting on it for three years." Patience transforms even the hardest situation.

**On group harmony:**
- 出る釘は打たれる (Deru kugi wa utareru): "The nail that sticks up gets hammered down." Conformity as protection—but also as conformity enforced.
- 沈黙は金 (Chinmoku wa kin): "Silence is golden." What you do NOT say is often more powerful.

**On impermanence:**
- 花は桜木、人は武士 (Hana wa sakuragi, hito wa bushi): "Among flowers the cherry, among men the samurai"—the most beautiful are those who accept death with grace.
- 物の哀れ (Mono no aware): Not a proverb but a concept: the bittersweet awareness of impermanence that makes beauty possible.

**On practical wisdom:**
- 急がば回れ (Isogaba maware): "If you're in a hurry, take the long way around." Counterintuitive efficiency.
- 猿も木から落ちる (Saru mo ki kara ochiru): "Even monkeys fall from trees." Experts make mistakes.

These proverbs often appear in manga, speeches, and daily conversation—knowing them reveals layers of meaning in Japanese communication.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1605407867590-40eb5a01eeac?w=800',
      tags: ['proverbs', 'wisdom', 'sayings', 'culture', 'philosophy'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 8),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_proverbs',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'japanese_numbers_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=94F5sTXhhjg',
      categoryId: 'language',
      title: 'Lucky and Unlucky Numbers in Japan',
      subtitle: 'Why hospitals skip floors 4 and 9, and why 8 makes businessmen smile',
      description: '''Numbers carry strong symbolic weight in Japanese culture, derived from how they sound (a phenomenon called goroawase—"number sound play") that affects everything from hospital floor numbering to business card pricing.

Unlucky numbers:
- **4 (shi/yon)**: Sounds like 死 (shi = death). Hospitals, hotels, and apartment buildings often skip floor 4. Some car manufacturers avoid 4s in model names for Japan
- **9 (ku/kyuu)**: Sounds like 苦 (ku = suffering/pain). Often avoided alongside 4 in gift sets—never give 9 items

Lucky numbers:
- **7 (shichi/nana)**: Lucky as in many cultures; 7-Eleven took advantage of this worldwide recognition
- **8 (hachi)**: The kanji 八 spreads wide at the bottom, symbolizing expanding fortune. Prices ending in ¥888 or license plates with 8888 are considered auspicious
- **3 (san)**: Sounds like 産 (san = birth/production). Also: "third time lucky" is a universal concept

Goroawase wordplay examples:
- 1192 (ichi-ichi-kyu-ni) = "ii kuni tsukurou" = "Let's make a good country" (Kamakura shogunate founding date, now a meme)
- 4649 (yo-ro-shi-ku) = "yoroshiku" = "nice to meet you / please treat me well"
- 0120 (o-i-ni-re-i) used in phone numbers for "please call us"

This number-sound association means phone numbers, prices, and addresses are constructed with deliberate meaning in Japan.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1607604276583-eef5d076aa5f?w=800',
      tags: ['numbers', 'lucky', 'superstition', 'language', 'culture'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 7),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_superstitions',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'body_language_japan_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gboLbnAJsl0',
      categoryId: 'language',
      title: 'Non-Verbal Communication in Japan',
      subtitle: 'The gestures, silences, and unspoken rules of Japanese social life',
      description: '''Japan is famously a "high-context culture"—meaning much of the real communication happens through context, relationship, and non-verbal signals rather than explicit speech. Understanding this changes how you see every interaction.

Key non-verbal signals:

**The bow:**
- 15 degrees: Casual acknowledgment or thanks
- 30 degrees: Standard formal greeting or gratitude
- 45 degrees: Deep formal greeting, apology, or meeting someone very important
- 90 degrees: Extremely sincere apology or reverence
- Reciprocal bowing can continue for several minutes as neither party wants to stop first

**Facial expression:**
- Japanese emotion display norms suppress strong expressions in public contexts
- A smile can mean happiness, embarrassment, discomfort, or an attempt to smooth tension
- Direct eye contact during serious conversation can feel confrontational rather than engaged

**Gesture signals:**
- Waving hand in front of face: "No thank you" / "I don't know"
- Pointing to one's own nose (not chest): "That's me" / "Do you mean me?"
- Crossing arms in an X: "No" / "Not available" / "Wrong"
- OK circle gesture (thumb and forefinger): Can mean "money" in Japan (the circle = coin)

**The concept of Ma (間):**
Silence in conversation is not awkward—it is thoughtful. Speaking immediately after someone finishes is slightly rude; pausing shows you are considering their words seriously.

**Tatemae vs. Honne:**
Public face (tatemae) and true feelings (honne) often differ significantly. "That might be difficult" (muzukashii desu ne) often means "absolutely not."''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1590559899731-a382839e5549?w=800',
      tags: ['non-verbal', 'bowing', 'body language', 'communication', 'social'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 6),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_etiquette',
    ),

