    CultureContent(
      id: 'bon_odori_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xsYUDJdlZ3k',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Obon: Welcoming the Spirits of the Dead',
      subtitle: 'Japan\'s summer festival of ancestral reunion',
      description: '''Obon is Japan's most important Buddhist observance—a four-day period (August 13-16) when the spirits of deceased ancestors are believed to return to visit the living, with the central festivities on August 13-15. It combines profound spiritual meaning with vibrant celebration.

The ritual sequence:
- **Mukae-bi (welcoming fire)**: On August 13, small fires are lit at the family gate to guide spirits home. Some regions use elaborate lanterns (chochin) hung outside
- **Obon festivities**: The middle days feature bon odori (bon dances)—circular dances held at temples and parks where participants wear yukata and dance together to the beat of taiko drums. The movements are simple and repetitive, meant to welcome the spirits
- **Okuri-bi (sending fire)**: On August 16, fires are lit again to guide spirits back to the spirit world

Famous Obon observations:
- **Kyoto's Daimonji**: Five enormous bonfires lit on mountain slopes, forming kanji characters visible across the city. The most famous forms the character 大 (large/great), using 75 fire pits spread across three hillside strokes
- **Nagasaki's Shoro-nagashi**: Families float elaborate paper boats (shoro) down rivers and into the sea, carrying the spirit home
- **Nishiki's Toro Nagashi**: Thousands of paper lanterns floated on water at dusk, creating an extraordinary visual

The bon odori dance differs by region—Awa Odori (Tokushima), Gujo Odori (Gifu, which runs all night), and Nishimonai Bon Odori (Akita, designated Intangible Cultural Heritage) each have distinct styles and costumes.

Many Japanese return to their family hometown during Obon—creating one of Japan's most congested travel periods.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1590559899731-a382839e5549?w=800',
      tags: ['obon', 'ancestors', 'festival', 'dance', 'summer'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 2),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obon',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'new_year_japan_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AmlkV22evnc',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Oshōgatsu: Japanese New Year',
      subtitle: 'Japan\'s most important holiday—quieter, deeper, and more spiritual than you expect',
      description: '''Japanese New Year (Oshōgatsu, January 1-3) is the country's most important holiday—but completely unlike Western or Chinese New Year celebrations. There are no fireworks or parties; instead, a profound quiet descends as Japan collectively pauses, purifies, and begins again.

New Year preparations (before midnight):
- **Osoji (big cleaning)**: The entire home is cleaned from ceiling to floors, removing the old year's energy
- **Kadomatsu**: Arrangements of pine, bamboo, and plum are placed at the entrance—welcoming the toshigami (year god) into the home
- **Osechi ryori**: Elaborate lacquerware boxes packed with symbolic foods, each carrying a meaning:
  - Tazukuri (dried sardines): Abundant harvest
  - Kuromame (black soybeans): Health and diligence
  - Kazunoko (herring roe): Fertility and prosperous descendants
  - Kohaku namasu (red and white daikon): Celebration

New Year activities:
- **Joya no Kane**: 108 bell strikes at midnight in Buddhist temples—each represents one of the 108 worldly desires that humans must release
- **Hatsumode**: First shrine visit of the year, typically within three days. Tens of millions make this pilgrimage; popular shrines receive millions of visitors
- **Fukubukuro**: "Lucky bags"—mystery bags sold by stores containing merchandise worth more than the price. Lines form before dawn
- **Otoshidama**: Cash gifts given to children in special envelopes by relatives

The food, rituals, and quiet of Oshōgatsu are deeply restorative—a collective reset for the entire nation.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1596040878161-bfeb61d7cec9?w=800',
      tags: ['new year', 'oshogatsu', 'shrine', 'tradition', 'osechi'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 1),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_New_Year',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'geisha_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N75LKZp7iUw',
      isPremium: true,
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Geisha: The Living Art of Entertainment',
      subtitle: 'Performers, not what you think—five years of training per art',
      description: '''Geisha are professional entertainers trained in traditional Japanese arts—music, dance, conversation, poetry, and the subtle art of making guests feel at ease. They are frequently misunderstood in Western media; understanding what geisha actually do reveals one of Japan's most extraordinary living traditions.

The training path:
- **Maiko** (apprentice geisha): Girls who have completed compulsory education enter an okiya (geisha house) and begin training, typically ages 15-20. They serve as assistants to senior geisha, learning by observation
- **Geiko** (full geisha; the Kyoto term): After 3-5 years of training, the maiko becomes a geiko in a ceremony called erikae ("turning of the collar"—from the red-and-white embroidered maiko collar to a plain white geiko collar)
- Arts studied: shamisen (three-string lute), Japanese flute, taiko drum, traditional dance (nihon buyo), tea ceremony, ikebana, calligraphy, and most importantly—the art of conversation

What geisha actually do:
- Perform at ozashiki (banquet parties) in exclusive ochaya (tea houses) in Kyoto's hanamachi (flower districts)
- Guests include politicians, executives, and the extremely wealthy
- Play word games, pour drinks, dance, perform music—creating an atmosphere of refined entertainment
- A single ozashiki costs hundreds of thousands of yen; geisha are accessible only through introduction

The five Kyoto hanamachi (geisha districts): Gion Kobu, Gion Higashi, Miyagawa-cho, Pontocho, and Kamishichiken. Early evening in Gion's Hanamikoji alley is the best chance to see maiko walking between engagements.

Current numbers: approximately 1,000 geisha remain in Japan, down from tens of thousands a century ago.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1624451579834-e0a46f8c56de?w=800',
      tags: ['geisha', 'maiko', 'kyoto', 'entertainment', 'traditional arts'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 31),
      readTime: 7,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geisha',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'japanese_etiquette_daily_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RJR2UTWbakg',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'The Invisible Rules of Daily Japanese Life',
      subtitle: 'Unspoken codes that keep 125 million people in harmony',
      description: '''Japan's social harmony is maintained by thousands of invisible rules that visitors may inadvertently break—not from rudeness, but from unfamiliarity. Understanding these rules reveals a fundamentally different approach to public life.

Transportation:
- **Train etiquette**: No phone calls on trains (texting is fine). Set phone to silent (manner mode). Offer seats to elderly, disabled, and pregnant passengers. Eat only on Shinkansen, never local trains
- **Priority seating**: Silver/pink seats near doors are reserved—even if the car is mostly empty, do not use them unless you need them
- **Standing in line**: Always queue. Jumping or cutting a line is one of the most serious social violations

Public behavior:
- **No eating while walking**: Eating is for seated situations. The exception is festival/market stalls where eating while walking is normal
- **Sorting garbage**: Japan has complex recycling rules—burnable, non-burnable, plastic, glass, aluminum are all separate. Many convenience stores have labeled bins; use them correctly
- **Escalators**: Stand on the left (in most of Japan; Osaka reverses this—stand right), walk on the left. This convention is absolute

At restaurants:
- **Oshibori**: The wet towel given at most restaurants is for cleaning your hands only—not your face
- **Splitting the bill (warikan)**: Dutch treat is common—often calculated by each person using the itemized receipt
- **Doggy bags**: Rare and slightly unusual to request; most food is meant to be finished

In shoes:
- Remove shoes at the entrance of homes, many traditional restaurants, some temples, and ryokan. Slippers are usually provided. Bathroom slippers are separate—never forget to change back.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1566073771259-6a8506099945?w=800',
      tags: ['etiquette', 'manners', 'daily life', 'social rules', 'customs'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 30),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_etiquette',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'bonsai_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DS_YvHvfrEI',
      isPremium: true,
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Bonsai: A Living Sculpture of Time',
      subtitle: 'How patience, philosophy, and a pair of scissors create a miniature universe',
      description: '''Bonsai (盆栽, "tray planting") is the art of growing miniature trees in containers, training them through pruning, wiring, and care to embody the beauty of ancient trees in a form you can hold in your hands.

What makes bonsai profound:
- A good bonsai evokes nature at its most powerful—a gnarled tree on a cliff edge, bent by centuries of wind—in miniature. The imagination completes what the eye sees
- Time is the medium: the trees are alive and growing. A master bonsai artist works with decades-long timescales, with the final "vision" realized after the artist's death
- The oldest bonsai specimens are over 1,000 years old, passed down through generations of masters

Core techniques:
- **Pruning (senjo)**: Removing branches to shape the tree, emphasizing certain directions of growth
- **Wiring**: Copper or aluminum wire wrapped around branches to bend them in slow curves over months; the wire is removed before it cuts into bark
- **Repotting**: Every 2-5 years, root pruning maintains miniature scale
- **Nebari**: Developing visible surface roots (nebari) that suggest an ancient tree—considered the most beautiful element

Famous styles:
- **Moyogi (informal upright)**: Trunk curves naturally, most common in nature
- **Shakan (slanting)**: Tree grows at 30-45 degree angle as if shaped by prevailing wind
- **Kengai (cascade)**: Trunk and branches cascade below the pot lip, suggesting a cliff tree
- **Shohin**: Miniature bonsai small enough to hold in one hand

The Omiya Bonsai Village (Saitama, near Tokyo) is Japan's bonsai capital with multiple galleries and the National Bonsai Museum.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1492571350019-22de08371fd3?w=800',
      tags: ['bonsai', 'trees', 'patience', 'art', 'nature'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 29),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonsai',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'sento_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMqyaggZKl8',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Sentō: The Neighborhood Public Bath',
      subtitle: 'Where Tokyo\'s oldest community life still happens',
      description: '''Long before private bathrooms became universal, the sentō (public bath) was the heart of Japanese neighborhood life. Today, surviving sentō are living museums of community connection and the art of the bath.

Sentō vs. Onsen:
- **Onsen**: Uses natural hot spring water; often in ryokan or resort settings
- **Sentō**: Heated tap water (sometimes with mineral additions); neighborhood facility, often running since Edo period

The sentō experience:
- Pay at the entrance (typically ¥490-520); rent a towel if you didn't bring one
- The changing room separates men and women; large mirrors, wooden lockers, and often an older caretaker who has worked there for decades
- The main bath room: a large tiled room with individual washing stations (small stool, two faucets, plastic bucket), a soaking tub (often 43-44°C), and sometimes a cold plunge, electric bath, or jet bath
- The tile paintings: Many old sentō feature large painted murals of Mt. Fuji on the wall above the baths—an Edo period tradition that made bathers feel they were bathing in nature

Why sentō matter:
- Conversation between neighbors happened naturally here—before television, before smartphones
- The calendar on the wall shows each neighborhood's designated bathing day for different age groups
- Many are disappearing: from over 2,600 sentō in Tokyo at their peak in the 1960s to under 450 today

Cultural revival: Young architects and designers are renovating historic sentō as design destinations while maintaining their ¥500 entry price.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1540569876013-4acf4a9bc1d5?w=800',
      tags: ['sento', 'public bath', 'neighborhood', 'community', 'bathing'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 28),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sent%C5%8D',
    ),

