    CultureContent(
      id: 'osaka_culture_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3sJNEyZ6Gu0',
      categoryId: 'region',
      title: 'Osaka: The Nation\'s Kitchen and Comedy Capital',
      subtitle: 'Where eating well is a civic virtue and laughter is mandatory',
      description: '''Osaka has its own identity, dialect, and civic pride that sets it apart from Tokyo's formality. Osakan culture prizes earthiness, humor, practicality, and above all—eating well. The city's motto is kuidaore: "eat until you drop."

What makes Osaka different:

**The accent and personality**: Osaka-ben (Kansai dialect) sounds melodic and emphatic compared to Tokyo Japanese. Osakans are famous for being funnier, more direct, and warmer with strangers—they'll start a conversation on the train.

**Tsukkomi and boke**: The foundational structure of Japanese comedy (manzai) was developed in Osaka. Tsukkomi (straight man who corrects) and boke (fool who misunderstands) is the template for virtually all Japanese comedic performance and has deeply influenced social interaction.

**Street food culture**:
- **Takoyaki**: Octopus balls cooked in special round molds, topped with mayonnaise, okonomiyaki sauce, bonito flakes, and aonori seaweed. Invented in Osaka in 1935, now Japan's most iconic street food
- **Okonomiyaki**: "Grilled as you like it"—a savory pancake/omelette of shredded cabbage, egg, flour, and whatever additions you choose (pork, seafood, cheese, mochi). Osaka style mixes everything; Hiroshima style layers ingredients
- **Kushikatsu**: Skewered and breaded deep-fried everything—vegetables, meats, cheese—with a shared dipping sauce. The absolute rule: never double-dip

**Dotonbori**: The neon-lit entertainment and food district along a canal; the enormous Glico running man sign is Osaka's iconic image. At night it vibrates with energy.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1566073771259-6a8506099945?w=800',
      tags: ['osaka', 'food', 'comedy', 'takoyaki', 'kuidaore'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 6, 2),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osaka',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'japanese_alps_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rf-oetrmaF4',
      categoryId: 'region',
      title: 'The Japanese Alps: Sacred Mountains and Mountain Culture',
      subtitle: 'Where pilgrimage, farming villages, and altitude meet',
      description: '''Japan's main island (Honshu) has a mountainous spine that concentrates Japan's most dramatic landscapes—the Japanese Alps, with peaks exceeding 3,000 meters. These mountains have shaped Japanese religion, aesthetics, and identity.

Mt. Fuji (富士山):
- 3,776 meters, Japan's highest peak and its most sacred mountain
- Visible from Tokyo on clear days (120 km away), it appears in thousands of artworks
- The climbing season is July-September; approximately 300,000 people climb annually
- Shinto belief: Fuji-san is the home of the fire goddess Konohanasakuya-hime; climbing it is an act of pilgrimage
- The "seventh station" (around 3,000m) is where altitude sickness begins for many climbers

Kamikochi (Nagano):
- No private cars allowed; accessed only by bus or on foot
- A pristine mountain valley with turquoise river, Japanese larch forests, and dramatic rock faces
- The Azusa River turns electric blue in spring from glacial melt
- Home to some of Japan's finest mountain climbing (Yari-ga-take, Oku-hotaka-dake)

Mountain villages:
- **Shirakawa-go**: Gassho-zukuri farmhouses designed to shed heavy snow; the steeply pitched thatched roofs are architectural engineering marvels
- **Tsumago/Magome**: Preserved post towns on the historic Nakasendo mountain road between Kyoto and Edo; connected by a 9 km hiking trail
- **Matsumoto**: Castle town with Japan's best-preserved original castle (Crow Castle, black lacquered walls), surrounded by the Alps

Mountain climbing culture: Japan's yama-girl (mountain girl) trend saw young women take up mountain climbing as fashion meets adventure.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1480796927426-f609979314bd?w=800',
      tags: ['mountains', 'fuji', 'alps', 'hiking', 'pilgrimage'],
      level: 2,
