    CultureContent(
      id: 'setsubun_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x8loudH5CwM',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Setsubun: Throwing Beans at Demons',
      subtitle: "February 3rd — Japan's New Year's Eve of the old calendar",
      description: '''Setsubun (節分) marks the eve of spring in Japan's old lunar calendar and is celebrated with one of the country's most entertaining rituals: throwing roasted soybeans while shouting "Oni wa soto! Fuku wa uchi!" (Out with demons! In with good luck!).

The bean-throwing ritual (mamemaki):
- The toshi-otoko (the man of the year, often the father) wears an oni mask while family members chase him through the house throwing beans
- One bean is eaten for each year of your age to ensure good health for the coming year
- Temples and shrines hold public mamemaki events where celebrities and sumo wrestlers throw bags of beans into large crowds

Why beans? In Japanese, the word for bean (mame) is a homophone for "hardworking/diligent" — and roasted beans can't sprout, symbolizing that the demons cannot take root.

Eho-maki: A more recent tradition (popularized by convenience stores in the 1980s and now nationwide) involves eating an entire uncut makizushi roll in silence while facing the "lucky direction" (determined each year by the Chinese zodiac). The roll must be consumed without speaking or putting it down.

Setsubun at famous temples:
- Narita-san Shinshoji Temple (Chiba): Famous for celebrities performing the bean throw
- Senso-ji (Tokyo Asakusa): Public ceremony with beans
- Yoshida Shrine (Kyoto): One of Japan's three major Setsubun ceremonies, including a ritual fire''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1590559899731-a382839e5549?w=800',
      tags: ['setsubun', 'festival', 'spring', 'beans', 'demons'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 28),
      readTime: 4,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Setsubun',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'tanabata_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8Khfv9go_f4',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Tanabata: The Star Festival',
      subtitle: 'Two stars, one night, a love story written in the Milky Way',
      description: '''Tanabata (七夕, "Evening of the Seventh") is celebrated on July 7th and tells the story of Orihime (the star Vega) and Hikoboshi (Altair) — two lovers separated by the Milky Way who are permitted to meet only once a year, when the stars align on Tanabata night.

The legend: Orihime, daughter of the Sky King, wove beautiful cloth beside the Milky Way. Her father worried she worked too hard, so he introduced her to Hikoboshi, a cowherd across the river. They fell deeply in love but neglected their work. The Sky King separated them across the Milky Way — unless it rains, which prevents the magpies from forming a bridge.

Tanabata traditions:
- **Tanzaku**: People write wishes on thin strips of colored paper (tanzaku) and hang them on bamboo branches along with origami decorations. At the end of the night, the bamboo and wishes are floated down rivers
- **Colors have meanings**: Yellow = wealth/gold; red = ancestors/gratitude; blue/green = growth/learning; pink = love/romance; purple = wisdom/study
- **Sending wishes to the stars**: The wishes are directed to Orihime and Hikoboshi

Famous Tanabata festivals:
- **Sendai Tanabata** (Miyagi): Japan's largest, held August 6-8; enormous paper decorations (kazarimono) hang from shopping arcades
- **Hiratsuka Tanabata** (Kanagawa): Second largest; bamboo decorations line the streets
- **Asagaya Tanabata** (Tokyo): Vibrant neighborhood festival with handmade decorations''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1480796927426-f609979314bd?w=800',
      tags: ['tanabata', 'stars', 'wishes', 'summer festival', 'legend'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 27),
      readTime: 4,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tanabata',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'hinamatsuri_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3_MsVRWVuf4',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Hinamatsuri: The Festival of Dolls',
      subtitle: 'A palace court of 15 dolls displayed once a year for daughters',
      description: '''Hinamatsuri (雛祭り, "Doll Festival") is celebrated on March 3rd to wish for the health and happiness of young girls. The centerpiece is a tiered display (hina kazari) of ornamental dolls dressed in Heian-period (794-1185) court clothing.

The display tiers (typically 5 or 7 levels, draped in red fabric):
- **1st tier**: Odairi-sama (Emperor) and Ohina-sama (Empress) — the most important dolls, dressed in formal court costume
- **2nd tier**: Three court ladies (san-nin kanjo)
- **3rd tier**: Five court musicians (go-nin bayashi)
- **4th tier**: Two ministers (right and left ministers)
- **5th tier**: Three servants

The dolls represent protection: in ancient Japan, paper dolls were floated down rivers to carry away evil spirits and illness. The Heian court stopped floating them and began displaying them instead, and the tradition has continued for centuries.

Timing matters: displays are traditionally put out after Setsubun (February 3rd)—from around February 4th through mid-February—and must be put away promptly after Hinamatsuri. Superstition holds that a girl who leaves her dolls out too long will be late to marry.

Food: Chirashizushi (scattered sushi), hishi mochi (diamond-shaped rice cakes in pink/white/green layers), amazake (sweet sake), and hina-arare (pastel-colored rice crackers) are traditional foods for the day.

Katsuura Hinamatsuri (Chiba): Famous for displaying dolls on outdoor temple steps, creating a cascading display of thousands of dolls.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1566073771259-6a8506099945?w=800',
      tags: ['hinamatsuri', 'dolls', 'girls', 'spring', 'Heian period'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 26),
      readTime: 4,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hinamatsuri',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'kodomo_no_hi_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lfQnv5_rQq4',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: "Kodomo no Hi: Children's Day and the Carp Streamers",
      subtitle: 'Why giant fish swim in the sky every May 5th',
      description: '''Kodomo no Hi (こどもの日, Children's Day) on May 5th is a national holiday celebrating all children's happiness and health. The most iconic image: tall bamboo poles with koinobori — carp-shaped cloth streamers flying in the wind.

Why carp? The koi (carp) symbolizes strength, courage, and determination in Japanese and Chinese culture. The koi swims powerfully upstream against the current — a model for children to overcome challenges. Koinobori sets always have:
- A black koi at the top (father)
- A red/pink koi (mother)
- Smaller blue, green, or orange koi for each child
- A spinning wheel at the top

Kabuto (samurai helmet) display: Like Hinamatsuri's doll display, families with boys display miniature samurai helmets (kabuto) and armor (yoroi), wishing for the courage and strength of a warrior. Traditionally this was called "Tango no Sekku" (Boy's Festival) and only celebrated boys — the name changed to "Children's Day" in 1948 to include everyone.

Chimaki and kashiwa mochi: Two foods specific to this day. Chimaki is sweet rice wrapped in bamboo leaves. Kashiwa mochi is rice cake wrapped in oak leaves — oaks don't drop their old leaves until new ones sprout, symbolizing that the family line will never die out.

Giant koinobori: The town of Kazo (Saitama) displays a 100-meter carp — one of the world's largest koinobori.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1492571350019-22de08371fd3?w=800',
      tags: ['kodomo no hi', 'koinobori', 'carp', 'children', 'May'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 25),
      readTime: 4,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Children%27s_Day_(Japan)',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'daruma_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HoADivIL1E0',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Daruma: The Tumbling Doll of Perseverance',
      subtitle: 'Draw one eye when you set a goal — the other when you achieve it',
      description: '''The Daruma doll (達磨) is Japan's most iconic symbol of perseverance and wish-granting. The round, red papier-mâché figure represents Bodhidharma (Daruma-daishi in Japanese) — the founder of Zen Buddhism, who legendarily sat in meditation for nine years until his legs withered away. The doll's weighted bottom makes it self-right when tipped, embodying the proverb: "Nana korobi ya oki" (Fall seven times, get up eight).

How to use a Daruma:
1. When setting a goal or making a wish, fill in ONE eye (usually left) with a black brush stroke
2. Place the doll in a visible location as a daily reminder
3. When the goal is achieved, fill in the SECOND eye — the Daruma is now "complete" and can be taken to a temple for burning (Daruma Kuyo)

Why one eye? There are multiple traditions — some say the maker left one eye blank so the doll would watch over the wish until fulfilled; others connect it to the Bodhidharma legend.

Daruma colors: Red is most common (good luck/warding off evil). But regional varieties and special-purpose Daruma use different colors: white (purity/success in exams), gold (financial luck), pink (love), black (warding off bad luck), and purple (health).

Political Daruma: Japanese politicians traditionally appear on election night with a Daruma, filling in the second eye if they win — broadcast live on national television. The image of a politician's Daruma eye ceremony is one of Japanese election coverage's defining moments.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1589710751893-f9a6770ad71b?w=800',
      tags: ['daruma', 'wishes', 'perseverance', 'Zen', 'luck'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 24),
      readTime: 4,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daruma_doll',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'maneki_neko_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TdVDqnu6z1I',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Maneki-Neko: The Beckoning Cat',
      subtitle: 'Why a cat with a raised paw sits at the entrance of every shop',
      description: '''The maneki-neko (招き猫, "beckoning cat") is one of Japan's most recognized symbols of good fortune. The ceramic cat with one paw raised welcomes customers, wealth, and luck — and can be found at the entrance of virtually every Japanese restaurant, shop, and business worldwide.

Which paw is raised?
- **Left paw raised**: Beckons people/customers inside (most common for businesses)
- **Right paw raised**: Beckons money and good fortune
- **Both paws**: Beckons both — though some say "both paws up means you can't defend yourself"

Color meanings:
- **White**: Purity, general good luck
- **Gold**: Financial prosperity (most common in modern mass-produced versions)
- **Black**: Warding off evil spirits
- **Red**: Protection from illness
- **Pink**: Love and romance
- **Green**: Academic success

The origin legend: Many stories, but the most popular involves a feudal lord sheltering under a tree during a storm. A cat from a nearby temple beckoned him inside. The moment he moved, lightning struck the tree where he'd been standing. The grateful lord patronized the temple; when the cat died, it was honored with a statue — the first maneki-neko.

Gotokuji Temple (Setagaya, Tokyo) claims to be this temple and displays hundreds of donated maneki-neko of all sizes — a powerful and unusual visual experience.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1547592180-85f173990554?w=800',
      tags: ['maneki-neko', 'lucky cat', 'shop', 'fortune', 'symbol'],
