    CultureContent(
      id: 'omamori_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lZ7CMEbEEEA',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Omamori: Charms That Protect You',
      subtitle: 'The small brocade pouches sold at every shrine and temple',
      description: '''Omamori (お守り, "protection charm") are small brocade pouches sold at Shinto shrines and Buddhist temples, each containing a paper or wooden prayer inscription (fuda) and believed to provide protection or blessing for a specific purpose.

Common types:
- **Kōtsū anzen**: Traffic safety — given to drivers, motorcyclists, cyclists
- **Gakugyō jōju**: Academic success — overwhelmingly popular during exam season
- **En-musubi**: Romantic ties / finding a partner
- **Kaiun**: Overall good luck
- **Kenko**: Health and recovery from illness
- **Anzan**: Safe childbirth — given to pregnant women
- **Shōbai hanjō**: Business success

The rule: An omamori from a specific shrine or temple is strongest for that deity's specialty. Buying a traffic safety omamori from a shrine famous for love is considered less effective than from one dedicated to protection.

How long do they last? One year is standard. Old omamori should be returned to the shrine for ritual burning (kito) at the new year — they should not be thrown in regular trash. The shrine returns the spirit of the charm to the deity.

Can you open it? Opening the pouch nullifies its power — the prayer inside is sacred. You keep the pouch sealed and display it or keep it in your bag, wallet, or car.

Popular omamori shrines: Ginkakuji for arts; Yushima Tenjin for exams; Meiji Jingu for general protection.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1566073771259-6a8506099945?w=800',
      tags: ['omamori', 'charm', 'shrine', 'protection', 'luck'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 22),
      readTime: 4,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omamori',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'omikuji_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lZ7CMEbEEEA',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Omikuji: Drawing Your Fortune at a Shrine',
      subtitle: 'A paper slip that tells your luck — and what to do with bad news',
      description: '''Omikuji (おみくじ, "sacred lot") are random fortune-telling strips drawn at shrines and temples — a ritual practiced by millions of Japanese people, especially during the New Year period and Hatsumode (first shrine visit).

How it works: You shake a hexagonal wooden box containing numbered sticks until one falls out, then take the paper fortune matching that number from a drawer. Or at modern shrines, select from a rack of folded paper.

Fortune levels (from best to worst):
1. **Dai-kichi** (大吉): Great blessing — excellent luck in all things
2. **Kichi** (吉): Blessing — general good luck
3. **Chū-kichi** (中吉): Middle blessing
4. **Shō-kichi** (小吉): Small blessing
5. **Han-kichi** (半吉): Half blessing
6. **Sue-kichi** (末吉): Future blessing (luck coming later)
7. **Kyō** (凶): Curse — bad luck
8. **Dai-kyō** (大凶): Great curse — the worst fortune

What the fortune covers: Health, love, business, travel, wishes, lost items, waiting for someone — each covered separately with specific predictions.

What to do with bad luck: Tie the paper to a pine tree (matsu) or wire rack near the shrine. "Tying" (musubi) the bad fortune to the tree leaves it there rather than carrying it home. Some people tie good fortunes too, to share the luck with the shrine.

The experience is participatory and charming — millions draw omikuji who don't consider themselves particularly religious.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1596040878161-bfeb61d7cec9?w=800',
      tags: ['omikuji', 'fortune', 'shrine', 'luck', 'ritual'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 21),
      readTime: 4,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omikuji',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'ema_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fto3lWz0QF8',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Ema: Writing Your Wish on a Wooden Plaque',
      subtitle: 'The votive plaques that cover shrine walls in layers of hopes',
      description: '''Ema (絵馬, "picture horse") are small wooden plaques purchased at shrines and temples. Visitors write a wish or prayer on the back, then hang the plaque at the shrine, leaving the wish with the deity.

The name: In ancient Japan, horses were considered sacred messengers of the gods. Live horses were donated to shrines for important prayers — but as this became impractical for ordinary people, clay, then paper, then wooden representations of horses were substituted. Over time the horse image remained while the plaque became the focus.

Modern ema: Usually pentagonal (resembling a house or stable roof), printed with the shrine's image or the current year's zodiac animal on one side. The reverse is left blank for writing. Prices range from ¥500 to ¥1,000.

What people write:
- Exam students: "Please let me pass my university entrance exam"
- Athletes: "Please let our team win the championship"
- The ill: Health prayers for self or loved ones
- Heartfelt: Marriage wishes, pregnancy hopes, safe childbirth
- Unusual: Modern shrines collect fascinating, sincere, sometimes humorous wishes

Famous ema collections: Kamakura Hachimangū is filled with student ema before exam season. Fushimi Inari's ema plaques line the thousands of fox (kitsune) messenger statues. Hokkaido's Hakusan Shrine sometimes displays year-end ema publicly.

Mass removal: After a year (or in January), ema are ritually burned at the shrine — the gods have read the prayers; the physical object has fulfilled its purpose.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1605407867590-40eb5a01eeac?w=800',
      tags: ['ema', 'wishes', 'shrine', 'prayer', 'wooden plaque'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 20),
      readTime: 4,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ema_(Shinto)',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'shichi_go_san_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ze1MtrplgNg',
      categoryId: 'culture',
      title: 'Shichi-Go-San: Celebrating 7, 5, and 3',
      subtitle: 'When children dress in kimono and visit shrines on November 15th',
      description: '''Shichi-Go-San (七五三, "Seven-Five-Three") is celebrated on November 15th for children who are turning or have turned 7 (girls), 5 (boys), and 3 (boys and girls). Dressed in elaborate kimono, children visit shrines with their families to pray for their health and continued growth.

Why those ages? In pre-modern Japan, child mortality was high; reaching these milestone ages was celebrated as evidence that the child would survive to adulthood. Each age had a specific coming-of-age ritual:
- **Age 3**: Kamioki ("hair-leaving") — boys and girls stop having their heads shaved and begin growing hair
- **Age 5**: Hakama-gi — boys wear hakama (formal pleated trousers) for the first time
- **Age 7**: Obi-toki — girls switch from cord-tied kimono to properly belted obi

The visual experience: Shichi-Go-San transforms shrine precincts in October and November into extraordinary displays — small children in formal kimono, some barely walking, being photographed by proud families. Professional photographers set up outside popular shrines.

Chitose-ame ("thousand-year candy"): Long, thin hard candy in red and white stripes, sold in bags decorated with cranes and turtles (symbols of longevity). Children carry these bags in their kimono sleeves — the candy's length symbolizes a long, healthy life.

The photo session: Many families rent kimono and hire professional photographers, creating elaborate portraits — one of Japan's most cherished family photography traditions.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1624451579834-e0a46f8c56de?w=800',
      tags: ['shichi-go-san', 'children', 'kimono', 'shrine', 'milestone'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 19),
      readTime: 4,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shichi-Go-San',
    ),

