    CultureContent(
      id: 'yakitori_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3UXXJpcwUzU',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Yakitori: The Art of the Skewer',
      subtitle: 'Every part of the chicken, grilled over charcoal — a craft in itself',
      description: '''Yakitori (焼き鳥, "grilled bird") is the Japanese art of skewering and grilling chicken pieces over charcoal — and the culture around it is far more sophisticated than "chicken on a stick" suggests.

The skewers: Authentic yakitori uses thin bamboo or steel skewers. Each piece is cut precisely to ensure even cooking — no random chunks, but measured uniform pieces.

Every part of the bird: A serious yakitori restaurant (known as a yakitori-ya) serves 10-20 different parts, each with its own name, texture, and preparation:
- **Momo** (thigh): Rich and flavorful, the most popular
- **Negima**: Alternating chicken and spring onion — the classic combination
- **Tsukune**: Minced chicken meatball, often with raw egg yolk for dipping
- **Kawa** (skin): Crisped to a crackling, often grilled twice
- **Hatsu** (heart): Small, firm, distinctive flavor
- **Reba** (liver): Served lightly cooked (pink inside) at quality restaurants
- **Seseri** (neck meat): Chewy, intensely flavored
- **Bonjiri** (tail): The fattiest cut — considered a delicacy

Seasoning: Two choices — tare (sweet soy glaze, repeatedly applied during grilling) or shio (salt only, for delicate cuts where the meat's own flavor is the point).

The charcoal: Binchotan charcoal (white charcoal from Wakayama) burns at a precise temperature and infuses a subtle smokiness without chemical flavor. High-end yakitori-ya use only binchotan.

Standing and drinking: Yakitori originated as working-class food under train tracks. The tradition of narrow yakitori alleys (yokocho) — tiny restaurants, standing at the counter, beer and yakitori — is one of Tokyo's great pleasures.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1547592180-85f173990554?w=800',
      tags: ['yakitori', 'grilled chicken', 'izakaya', 'charcoal', 'skewer'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 28),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yakitori',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'shabu_shabu_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FE1JByjbo5E',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Shabu-Shabu & Sukiyaki: Hot Pot Rituals',
      subtitle: 'Cooking together at the table — where food becomes a social event',
      description: '''Japan's two great hot-pot traditions — shabu-shabu and sukiyaki — transform the dinner table into a communal cooking experience where conversation and food are inseparable.

SHABU-SHABU:
Named for the sound of meat swished through hot water (shab-shab). Thin slices of beef (or pork) are held with chopsticks and swirled briefly through a pot of kombu dashi broth — just 3-5 seconds for high-quality wagyu, longer for leaner cuts. Vegetables, tofu, and mushrooms simmer in the broth while you eat. Two dipping sauces: ponzu (citrus-soy) and goma-dare (sesame). The broth becomes progressively richer as the meal continues; it's drunk as soup at the end.

SUKIYAKI:
Richer and sweeter. Thin beef slices are pan-fried in a cast iron pot (nabe) with sugar, soy sauce, sake, and mirin, then cooked alongside vegetables. Before eating, each piece is briefly dipped in raw beaten egg — the egg clings and cooks slightly on the hot meat, creating a luxurious coating. The sweet-salty broth is considered even better than the meat.

Regional differences: Kanto (Tokyo) style adds the warishita sauce (pre-mixed sweet soy) all at once. Kansai (Osaka) style sugars and sears the beef first, then adds liquid — creating a stronger caramelization.

All-you-can-eat hot pot (食べ放題): Budget-friendly chain restaurants (Momo Paradise, Nabezo) offer unlimited meat and vegetables for ¥2,000-3,000 — one of Japan's best value dining experiences.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1569758269042-a9e3b38be36e?w=800',
      tags: ['shabu-shabu', 'sukiyaki', 'hot pot', 'wagyu', 'social'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 27),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shabu-shabu',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'tonkatsu_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qz1hnBPJD_g',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Tonkatsu: The Breaded Cutlet That Conquered Japan',
      subtitle: 'A Western technique, a Japanese ingredient, and 120 years of perfecting it',
      description: '''Tonkatsu (豚カツ, "pork cutlet") is Japan's adaptation of the Western breaded cutlet — introduced in the Meiji era (late 1800s) when Japan opened to Western food, then transformed into something distinctly Japanese. It is now one of Japan's most beloved comfort foods, with dedicated tonkatsu restaurants serving it as an art form.

The anatomy of a perfect tonkatsu:
- **The cut**: Rosu (loin, with a band of fat on one edge) or hire (tenderloin, leaner). The choice determines flavor vs. tenderness
- **The prep**: Meat is lightly scored, pounded to even thickness, salted, dredged in flour, dipped in egg, then coated in panko (Japanese breadcrumbs — coarser and crunchier than Western breadcrumbs)
- **The fry**: In deep oil at 160-170°C for 5-6 minutes; then a second fry at 180°C for 1-2 minutes to crisp the crust while keeping the inside juicy. The double-fry is the key
- **The cut**: Sliced into strips after frying — the crosswise cut exposes the interior and makes chopstick use possible

The sauce: Tonkatsu sauce (a thick, fruity-spicy condiment made from vegetables, fruit, and spices) is applied at the table. Quality restaurants offer their own house sauce, sometimes aged.

Katsu variations: Katsudon (tonkatsu simmered with egg over rice, one of Japan's most beloved donburi), katsu curry (tonkatsu on curry rice), katsusando (tonkatsu in a fluffy white bread sandwich, beloved as a Shinkansen bento).

Luck before exams: Because "katsu" sounds like "to win/overcome" (勝つ), eating tonkatsu before important exams or sports matches is considered good luck.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1585032226651-759b368d7246?w=800',
      tags: ['tonkatsu', 'pork', 'breaded', 'comfort food', 'panko'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 26),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tonkatsu',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'oden_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3blIJ8n1MHg',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Oden: Winter in a Bowl',
      subtitle: 'Japan\'s slow-simmered cold-weather comfort — now available 24/7',
      description: '''Oden (おでん) is the definitive winter comfort food of Japan — a simmering pot of dashi broth filled with a rotating cast of ingredients, each absorbing flavor over hours of low, gentle cooking.

What's in oden: The selection varies by region and cook, but common ingredients include:
- **Daikon** (white radish): The soul of oden — slow cooking transforms it to silky softness that melts when poked
- **Chikuwa** (fish cake tube): Light, bouncy, absorbs broth
- **Konnyaku** (konjac): Rubbery, almost black, virtually calorie-free — a textural counterpoint
- **Atsuage** (fried tofu block): Soaks up broth like a sponge
- **Hanpen**: Fluffy white fish cake, almost like a cloud — uniquely Tokyo
- **Tamago** (boiled egg): Simmered until the white turns brown from soy
- **Gyūsuji** (beef tendon): Melting, gelatinous, deeply savory
- **Kinchaku** (mochi in fried tofu pouch): The most beloved item for many

The broth: Each restaurant has its own dashi recipe, often built over years. Some keep a "mother broth" that is never fully replaced — only topped up. The broth is the heart of oden; tasting it tells you everything.

Regional differences: Kanto style uses dark soy broth. Kansai uses lighter dashi. Nagoya adds red miso. Shizuoka uses black broth made from beef tendons and black fish paste.

Convenience store oden: Japan's convenience stores (7-Eleven, Lawson, FamilyMart) serve hot oden from October through March — you select your own ingredients and are handed a cup of broth. Unassuming but genuinely good.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1547592180-85f173990554?w=800',
      tags: ['oden', 'winter', 'stew', 'comfort food', 'dashi'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 25),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oden',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'bento_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E-oiq5kApBg',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Bento: The Lunch Box as Japanese Art Form',
      subtitle: 'Nutrition, aesthetics, efficiency, and love — in one box',
      description: '''The bento (弁当) is Japan's packed lunch — but saying "packed lunch" understates it. At its best, a bento is a carefully balanced composition of nutrition, color, texture, and flavor arranged with genuine aesthetic consideration.

The rule of five: Traditional bento applies Japanese cooking principles — five colors (red, yellow, green, black/dark, white), five tastes (sweet, salty, sour, bitter, umami), five cooking methods in one box.

Standard bento components:
- **Rice** (or sometimes noodles): The base, occupying roughly half the box
- **Protein**: Karaage chicken, tamagoyaki (rolled omelette), grilled fish, tonkatsu
- **Vegetables**: A mixture of colors — edamame for green, cherry tomato for red, pickled plum for tart contrast
- **Decoration**: Nori (seaweed) cut into shapes, food dividers (baran), wax paper cups

Kyaraben (character bento): An entire subculture where parents (usually mothers) transform bento into cartoon characters, landscapes, anime scenes, or animals using food. Competitions and books are dedicated to this art form.

Ekiben (station bento): Sold at train stations throughout Japan, each regional ekiben highlights local specialties — crab in Hokkaido, makunouchi rice in Tokyo, takenoko (bamboo shoot) in Kyoto. Collecting ekiben is a travel hobby; dedicated ekiben fairs are held at department stores.

Convenience store bento: Surprisingly sophisticated. Japan's konbini (convenience stores) sell hundreds of bento varieties daily, freshly prepared in central kitchens and delivered multiple times per day.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1569718212165-3a8278d5f624?w=800',
      tags: ['bento', 'lunch box', 'aesthetics', 'ekiben', 'meal prep'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 24),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bento',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'natto_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=quM18413pOg',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Natto: The Fermented Soybean That Divides Japan',
      subtitle: 'Slimy, pungent, deeply nutritious — and fiercely loved by half the country',
      description: '''Natto (納豆) is fermented soybeans — arguably Japan's most polarizing food, even among Japanese people. Strongly associated with the Kanto region (Tokyo/eastern Japan), it is consumed daily by millions and avoided entirely by many others.

What natto is: Soybeans fermented with Bacillus subtilis bacteria for 24 hours at 40°C. The process creates an extraordinary texture — a stringy, sticky web of "natto threads" (neba-neba) that stretches when the beans are stirred or lifted. The smell is distinctively pungent, often described as "ammonia-like" by those who dislike it.

Taste profile: Savory, slightly bitter, earthy, deeply umami. The fermentation creates glutamic acid naturally, giving natto an intense savory depth.

Why Japanese people eat it:
- **Nutrition**: Natto is extraordinarily nutritious. It is high in protein, vitamin K2 (one of the highest dietary sources — crucial for bone health), nattokinase (an enzyme believed to prevent blood clots), and probiotics
- **Breakfast tradition**: Natto over rice is the definitive Japanese breakfast for millions — simple, complete, fast
- **Regional identity**: Kanto people eat it daily; many Kansai and Kyushu people refuse it entirely

How to eat it: Stir vigorously (this creates more threads and develops flavor), add the included soy sauce packet and karashi mustard, pour over hot rice. Chopped green onion and raw egg yolk are popular additions.

The regional divide: A famous Japanese television survey found that Kanto residents ate natto several times per week on average, while Osaka residents rarely or never ate it — a division that is itself a cultural identity marker.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1547592180-85f173990554?w=800',
      tags: ['natto', 'fermented', 'soybeans', 'breakfast', 'health'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 23),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natt%C5%8D',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'tofu_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2_cNqdv7dxM',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Tofu: Japan\'s Most Misunderstood Ingredient',
      subtitle: 'Not a meat substitute — a delicate, versatile food with a 2,000-year history',
      description: '''In Japan, tofu is not a substitute for anything. It is a primary ingredient — delicate, nuanced, and consumed fresh in ways that export tofu (designed to survive weeks in packages) cannot replicate.

Making tofu: Soybeans are soaked, ground, and heated to produce soy milk. A coagulant (nigari — natural magnesium chloride from seawater, or gypsum) is added to solidify the protein. The curd is placed in molds and pressed to varying degrees of firmness.

Tofu types:
- **Silken tofu (kinu-dofu)**: Not pressed; the curd sets directly in the container. Extremely delicate, almost pudding-like. Used for fresh eating, soups, desserts
- **Firm tofu (momen-dofu)**: Pressed; holds its shape. Denser, chewier. Used for stir-fry, deep-frying, simmered dishes
- **Abura-age**: Thin-sliced tofu deep-fried twice until hollow inside; used in miso soup, stuffed with rice (inari sushi), or as a pocket for other fillings
- **Atsuage**: Thick-cut tofu deep-fried; crispy outside, soft inside
- **Yuba**: The "skin" that forms on the surface of heating soy milk — delicate, nutty, considered a delicacy; fresh yuba in Kyoto is extraordinary

How to eat fresh tofu: Hiyayakko (cold tofu) — silken tofu served cold with nothing but katsuobushi (bonito flakes), grated ginger, spring onion, and a few drops of soy sauce. This is fresh tofu eaten at its best.

Tofu cuisine (Kyoto): "Yudofu" — tofu simmered at the table in kombu broth, dipped in ponzu. This seemingly simple dish showcases the quality of the tofu itself.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1569718212165-3a8278d5f624?w=800',
      tags: ['tofu', 'soy', 'vegetarian', 'traditional', 'Kyoto'],
      level: 2,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 22),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tofu',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'japanese_breakfast_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zRIDcCLxdRI',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'The Japanese Breakfast: A Complete Meal Before 8am',
      subtitle: 'Miso soup, rice, fish, pickles — the world\'s most nutritious morning routine',
      description: '''The traditional Japanese breakfast (asa-gohan, "morning rice") is arguably the world's most nutritious way to start the day — and the antithesis of cereal or toast.

The components of a full Japanese breakfast:
- **Gohan** (rice): The center of every meal. Short-grain Japanese rice, freshly cooked, served in a small wooden bowl
- **Miso soup**: Made fresh each morning. The combination of dashi, miso paste, and seasonal vegetables or tofu provides protein, probiotics, and umami
- **Yakizakana** (grilled fish): A small piece of salted and grilled fish — mackerel (saba), salmon (sake), or dried horse mackerel (aji no himono). The simplest preparation is always the standard
- **Tamagoyaki**: Rolled omelette, gently sweet, in rectangular slices
- **Pickles** (tsukemono): A small dish of one or two varieties — the acid and crunch balance the meal
- **Natto**: For eastern Japan households; served alongside rice
- **Nori**: Dried seaweed to wrap rice
- **A cup of green tea**

This combination covers: complex carbohydrates, complete protein, probiotics (miso, pickles), omega-3 fatty acids (fish), vitamins from seaweed — all in one compact meal.

The convenience store version: Japan's konbini also sell all these components individually for self-assembly, making this nutritious breakfast accessible on the go.

Ryokan breakfast: Japan's traditional inns (ryokan) serve elaborate versions of the traditional breakfast with regional specialties — widely considered the most memorable meal you can have in Japan.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1547592180-85f173990554?w=800',
      tags: ['breakfast', 'miso', 'rice', 'fish', 'ryokan'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 21),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_cuisine',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'yakisoba_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u5054SnMuaI',
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Yakisoba & Street Festival Food Culture',
      subtitle: 'The food stalls (yatai) that turn every festival into a feast',
      description: '''Yakisoba (焼きそば, "fried noodles") is Japan's definitive festival street food — wheat noodles stir-fried with pork, cabbage, and carrots on an enormous iron griddle, sauced with a sweet-savory Worcestershire-style sauce and topped with aonori (green seaweed powder) and pickled ginger.

The yatai (屋台): Mobile food stalls that set up at festivals, temple fairs, and neighborhood events. The sound, smell, and sight of yatai are inseparable from Japanese matsuri (festival) culture:
- **Yakisoba**: The most popular
- **Takoyaki**: Osaka's octopus balls, cooked in round molds
- **Karaage**: Juicy fried chicken pieces in paper cones
- **Choco banana**: Bananas dipped in chocolate and covered in sprinkles — a festival-only experience
- **Kakigori**: Shaved ice with syrup
- **Ayu no shio-yaki**: Salted whole river fish on skewers, roasted over charcoal
- **Taiyaki**: Fish-shaped waffles filled with anko (red bean paste)
- **Wataame (cotton candy)**: Spun to enormous size

Festival atmosphere: Yatai culture is about the experience as much as the food — the orange glow of lanterns, the sound of the griddle sizzle, the crowds in yukata. Portions are not enormous; the eating is leisurely and social.

Hakata yatai (Fukuoka): Fukuoka maintains Japan's most vibrant permanent yatai culture — dozens of outdoor food stalls operate nightly along the river, serving ramen, gyoza, and local dishes. A UNESCO-recognized living food culture.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1596040878161-bfeb61d7cec9?w=800',
      tags: ['yakisoba', 'festival', 'yatai', 'street food', 'matsuri'],
      level: 1,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 20),
      readTime: 5,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yakisoba',
    ),

    CultureContent(
      id: 'japanese_knife_01',
      videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6b5RFay7yU4',
      isPremium: true,
      categoryId: 'food',
      title: 'Japanese Kitchen Knives: Precision Forged Over Centuries',
      subtitle: 'The same tradition as samurai swords, applied to the kitchen',
      description: '''Japanese kitchen knives (hocho) are considered among the world's finest cutting tools — a tradition deeply intertwined with sword-making, sharing forging techniques and steel-working knowledge developed over centuries. Hocho have been central to Japanese cooking since the Heian period; after the 1876 Meiji-era Haitōrei Edict banned the wearing of swords, many swordsmiths redirected their craft entirely to kitchen knives, further refining and expanding the tradition.

What makes Japanese knives different:
- **Steel hardness**: Japanese knives use harder steel (62-67 HRC on the Rockwell scale) than German knives (56-58 HRC). Harder steel holds a sharper edge longer but is more brittle — requires more careful use
- **Thinner blade**: A Japanese chef's knife is thinner and lighter, allowing more precise cuts
- **Single bevel vs. double bevel**: Traditional Japanese knives are single-bevel (sharpened on one side only) — producing an extraordinarily sharp edge but requiring different technique. Double-bevel knives (sharpened both sides) are common in modern professional kitchens

Knife types for different tasks:
- **Gyuto**: Japanese chef's knife, Western-influenced, double bevel — the most versatile
- **Yanagiba**: Long, thin, single-bevel — for slicing raw fish (sashimi). The hallmark of sushi chefs
- **Deba**: Heavy, single-bevel — for breaking down whole fish, cutting through bone
- **Santoku**: "Three virtues" (meat, fish, vegetables) — shorter, wider than gyuto, the most common home-kitchen knife
- **Nakiri**: Rectangular vegetable cleaver — for precise, vertical vegetable cuts
- **Usuba**: Single-bevel equivalent of nakiri — the traditional Japanese vegetable knife

Steel types: Hagane (high-carbon steel, extremely sharp but rusts), stainless steel (more maintenance-tolerant), and Damascus (layered steel, combining hardness with rust resistance).

Sakai (Osaka) and Seki (Gifu) are Japan's knife-making capitals.''',
      imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1578301978162-7aae4d755dd5?w=800',
      tags: ['knife', 'craftsmanship', 'steel', 'cooking', 'sushi'],
      level: 3,
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 5, 19),
      readTime: 6,
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_kitchen_knives',
    ),

