    didYouKnow: 'The decline of the nengajō (New Year postcard) is one of the clearest quantitative measures of changing Japanese social practices. Peak nengajō sending was approximately 4.5 billion postcards in 2003; by 2023 it had declined to approximately 1.4 billion — a 69% reduction in 20 years. The decline tracks almost exactly with the rise of LINE (the dominant Japanese messaging app), smartphone penetration among older demographics, and the specific timing of major social shifts: 2011\'s earthquake and tsunami prompted many people to reconsider the obligatory nature of their social networks, and many chose not to resume nengajō exchanges afterward. Japan Post, which depends on nengajō for substantial December-January revenue (the nengajō is the postal product with the highest volume in Japan), has responded with digital-physical hybrid products and aggressive marketing. But the trend is demographically driven — younger Japanese people find nengajō formal and labor-intensive compared to a LINE message, and the question of which relationships still warrant the physical card has forced people to consciously evaluate the nature of their social networks in ways that informal continuation of the custom did not.',
